window.PLUMBLINE_DATA = {"project": "The Divine Plumbline", "projectKey": "divine-plumbline", "version": "1.4.0", "built": "2026-11-17 15:52", "builtISO": "2026-11-17T15:52:38+01:00", "parts": [{"id": "O.1", "title": "Overview — The Divine Plumbline in Five Days", "order": 0, "metadata": {"kind": "overview", "summarizes": "P.1 and D.1–D.5"}, "file": "o1-overview.md", "notes": [], "blocks": [{"ref": "O.1 §0 ¶0", "anchor": "p-O.1-0-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "ddf39d3bff", "text": "Overview — The Divine Plumbline in Five Days"}, {"ref": "O.1 §0 ¶1", "anchor": "p-O.1-0-1", "kind": "paragraph", "h": "dbfdad167e", "text": "A short summary of the five-day Divine Plumbline week, drawn from the plan (P.1) and the day texts (D.1–D.5). Each day has two parts of about 1.5 hours: Part 1 in the morning, Part 2 in the afternoon. Personal story slides are listed, not summarized; \"not covered\" means the story is still a placeholder."}, {"ref": "O.1 §1 ¶0", "anchor": "p-O.1-1-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "faf4ce7100", "text": "Day 1 — Welcome & The Plumbline / Truth and Deception"}, {"ref": "O.1 §1 ¶1", "anchor": "p-O.1-1-1", "kind": "paragraph", "h": "6407d1af46", "text": "Read Day 1 in full"}, {"ref": "O.1 §2 ¶0", "anchor": "p-O.1-2-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "71ea33c5f0", "text": "Part 1 — Welcome & The Plumbline"}, {"ref": "O.1 §2 ¶1", "anchor": "p-O.1-2-1", "kind": "paragraph", "h": "d7e991db99", "text": "The week opens in prayer, with an honest, relational tone. Amos 7:7-9 introduces the plumbline: an absolute, unmoving standard. It is our behavior, not our personality, that is built like a wall, brick by protective brick; repeated long enough, it becomes a spiritual stronghold (2 Corinthians 10:4-5). The true plumbline to measure against is Christ. Then the four identity questions (Ontology, Epistemology, Teleology, Axiology), the Belief Tree, a world views survey, and the biblical \"heart\" as the whole inner person (Proverbs 4:23)."}, {"ref": "O.1 §2 ¶2", "anchor": "p-O.1-2-2", "kind": "list", "h": "6ae1bb4523", "text": "Exercise: Icebreaker \"Growing Up\" (Slide 5) Stories: Slides 1, 8 written (Review 1); personal, not summarized"}, {"ref": "O.1 §3 ¶0", "anchor": "p-O.1-3-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "12af2153a5", "text": "Part 2 — Truth and Deception"}, {"ref": "O.1 §3 ¶1", "anchor": "p-O.1-3-1", "kind": "paragraph", "h": "d878eff20e", "text": "Who has been telling us what to see: the seven false prophets (Parents, Teachers, Peers, Government, Media, Church, the Heart itself). None is evil by definition, but each becomes a false prophet when it replaces God's voice on who we are. Satan as the ultimate deceiver (Amos 5:19) plays the long game of distorted identity. Storms of life do not create the wall; they reveal it. Closes with the Day 1 recap and questions."}, {"ref": "O.1 §3 ¶2", "anchor": "p-O.1-3-2", "kind": "list", "h": "2d50e28b0f", "text": "Exercise: \"Which Storm Revealed Your Wall?\", quiet journaling (Slide 15) Stories (not covered): Slides 12, 13"}, {"ref": "O.1 §4 ¶0", "anchor": "p-O.1-4-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "6238fbf296", "text": "Day 2 — Love Deficit & the Generational Pattern / The Two Walls and the Four Situations"}, {"ref": "O.1 §4 ¶1", "anchor": "p-O.1-4-1", "kind": "paragraph", "h": "178a1ad445", "text": "Read Day 2 in full"}, {"ref": "O.1 §5 ¶0", "anchor": "p-O.1-5-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "f7e69995f5", "text": "Part 1 — Love Deficit and the Generational Pattern"}, {"ref": "O.1 §5 ¶1", "anchor": "p-O.1-5-1", "kind": "paragraph", "h": "02f6cdfca1", "text": "When the need to be loved, valued and secure is not met, an identity crisis begins. The Love Deficit equation; visiting vs. blessing (Exodus 20:5-6), with sour grapes as its picture (Ezekiel 18:2); and the generational symptom chain from the book, where childhood reactions harden into adult symptoms. The same deficit leads down two roads: Rejection (\"I am not enough\") and Rebellion (\"I will make myself enough\"). Through Jesus' atonement we can break our agreement with what was spoken over us."}, {"ref": "O.1 §5 ¶2", "anchor": "p-O.1-5-2", "kind": "list", "h": "5b3e609a45", "text": "Exercise: \"Where Do You Feel the Deficit?\", move to a corner of the room (Slide 5) Stories (not covered): Slides 3, 7"}, {"ref": "O.1 §6 ¶0", "anchor": "p-O.1-6-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "49d576f8b0", "text": "Part 2 — The Two Walls and the Four Situations"}, {"ref": "O.1 §6 ¶1", "anchor": "p-O.1-6-1", "kind": "paragraph", "h": "295b693fa1", "text": "The Wall of Rejection (cornerstone: unbelief) and the Wall of Rebellion (cornerstone: pride), each with its brick inventory by Emotions, Intellect and Spirit. Everyone takes the Divine Plumbline Questionnaire (24 items, True/False) and scores it before the reveal. Then the four typical situations and their behaviors: O Compliant (Saul), U Can't Do It (Moses), W Competitive (Jacob), C Critical (Judas). They are not mutually exclusive: a high score names a wall you built, not who you are."}, {"ref": "O.1 §6 ¶2", "anchor": "p-O.1-6-2", "kind": "list", "h": "20f2fee237", "text": "Exercise: \"Both Walls\" diagram walkthrough & scoring (Slide 17) Stories (not covered): Slide 15"}, {"ref": "O.1 §7 ¶0", "anchor": "p-O.1-7-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "b2eea0ac02", "text": "Day 3 — The Lust Trap (Flight Swing) / The Pride Trap (Fight Swing) and the Final Swing"}, {"ref": "O.1 §7 ¶1", "anchor": "p-O.1-7-1", "kind": "paragraph", "h": "d589f5365f", "text": "Read Day 3 in full"}, {"ref": "O.1 §8 ¶0", "anchor": "p-O.1-8-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "d432f953e8", "text": "Part 1 — The Lust Trap (Flight Swing)"}, {"ref": "O.1 §8 ¶1", "anchor": "p-O.1-8-1", "kind": "paragraph", "h": "1d2ce0f939", "text": "Behind the Wall of Rejection: \"lust\" in a broad sense, any craving used to numb or escape pain, which deepens the wound it tries to escape. The Flight Swing moves between numbing/indulgence and self-punishment/withdrawal. Eating disorders serve as a case study (pastoral tone), and Solomon's Opponent-Process Theory explains why the swing grows more extreme."}, {"ref": "O.1 §8 ¶2", "anchor": "p-O.1-8-2", "kind": "list", "h": "ba7eac7563", "text": "Exercise: \"Naming the Swing\", quiet reflection and stretch (Slide 5) Stories (not covered): Slide 4"}, {"ref": "O.1 §9 ¶0", "anchor": "p-O.1-9-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "84b1f7def0", "text": "Part 2 — The Pride Trap (Fight Swing) and the Final Swing"}, {"ref": "O.1 §9 ¶1", "anchor": "p-O.1-9-1", "kind": "paragraph", "h": "5aef1ed6c7", "text": "Behind the Wall of Rebellion: pride as self-sufficiency. The Fight Swing moves between control/domination and collapse/burnout, and secular humanism's \"Superman Philosophy\" can quietly reinforce it. The Final Swing: left unaddressed, both swings can converge toward the same destructive end, a kind of double-mindedness (a warning, not a diagnosis). Awareness is not enough; restoration is needed."}, {"ref": "O.1 §9 ¶2", "anchor": "p-O.1-9-2", "kind": "list", "h": "8c267bab8a", "text": "Exercise: \"Spot the Superman Message\", group activity (Slide 15) Stories (not covered): Slide 12"}, {"ref": "O.1 §10 ¶0", "anchor": "p-O.1-10-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "75d60cb427", "text": "Day 4 — Restoration Begins: Revelation and Repentance / Release, Recognize, and Renounce"}, {"ref": "O.1 §10 ¶1", "anchor": "p-O.1-10-1", "kind": "paragraph", "h": "e0d9f1b130", "text": "Read Day 4 in full"}, {"ref": "O.1 §11 ¶0", "anchor": "p-O.1-11-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "6716185dce", "text": "Part 1 — Restoration Begins: Revelation and Repentance"}, {"ref": "O.1 §11 ¶1", "anchor": "p-O.1-11-1", "kind": "paragraph", "h": "9324306743", "text": "The tone shifts from diagnosis to restoration: the Wall of Salvation. First the altar and the temple (worship before structural repair), then two images of how God works: gold refined by fire and clay shaped by the Potter. He doesn't leave us to ourselves. An overview of the seven reinforcing rods, then Rod 1, Revelation (exposing to heal, not to shame), and Rod 2, Repentance (turning from unbelief or from pride), closing with a short written repentance sentence."}, {"ref": "O.1 §11 ¶2", "anchor": "p-O.1-11-2", "kind": "list", "h": "e33933b723", "text": "Exercise: \"Naming One Brick\", private (Slide 6) Stories (not covered): Slide 5"}, {"ref": "O.1 §12 ¶0", "anchor": "p-O.1-12-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "e2628a9153", "text": "Part 2 — Release, Recognize, and Renounce"}, {"ref": "O.1 §12 ¶1", "anchor": "p-O.1-12-1", "kind": "paragraph", "h": "9bc3977c03", "text": "Rod 3, Release: forgiveness as releasing a debt, a decision rather than a feeling, with a private list of names as preparation for Personal Ministry Time. Rods 4–5: recognizing the enemy's tactics and renouncing them with an active, spoken break, practiced first as a group with a light example. Rods 1–5 are tied together, and the session sets up Personal Ministry Time."}, {"ref": "O.1 §12 ¶2", "anchor": "p-O.1-12-2", "kind": "list", "h": "ea735973ce", "text": "Exercise: Rods 4–5 Recognize and Renounce, stand-and-move (Slide 15) Stories (not covered): Slide 14"}, {"ref": "O.1 §13 ¶0", "anchor": "p-O.1-13-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "f4903a355e", "text": "Day 5 — Renewing the Mind / Rebuilding, the Gates of Jerusalem, and Commissioning"}, {"ref": "O.1 §13 ¶1", "anchor": "p-O.1-13-1", "kind": "paragraph", "h": "cf25ef77db", "text": "Read Day 5 in full"}, {"ref": "O.1 §14 ¶0", "anchor": "p-O.1-14-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "bd37fd4cba", "text": "Part 1 — Renewing the Mind"}, {"ref": "O.1 §14 ¶1", "anchor": "p-O.1-14-1", "kind": "paragraph", "h": "07f1826b85", "text": "Rod 6, Renewing (Romans 12:2): an ongoing replacement of old thought patterns with truth, in eight steps: Acknowledging, Believing, Confessing, Desiring, Exercising (the A-C-D-B model), Forgiving, Growing, Helping. Includes writing one \"confession\" statement that replaces an old belief with a biblical truth."}, {"ref": "O.1 §14 ¶2", "anchor": "p-O.1-14-2", "kind": "list", "h": "7f657bf90f", "text": "Exercise: \"Practice the A-C-D-B Loop\", together and then privately (Slide 7) Stories (not covered): Slides 4, 9"}, {"ref": "O.1 §15 ¶0", "anchor": "p-O.1-15-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "175a8b5204", "text": "Part 2 — Rebuilding, the Gates of Jerusalem, and Commissioning"}, {"ref": "O.1 §15 ¶1", "anchor": "p-O.1-15-1", "kind": "paragraph", "h": "e05d037dad", "text": "Rod 7, Rebuilding: a new wall laid on the true plumbline (Christ), following Nehemiah. The ten gates of Jerusalem as an allegory of restored life (Sheep, Fish, Old, Valley, Rubbish, Fountain, Water, Horse, East, Watch), and the King of Glory (Psalm 24), with a quiet look back at the Day 2 questionnaire results. The week closes with a full recap, testimonies and encouragement, and a commissioning prayer."}, {"ref": "O.1 §15 ¶2", "anchor": "p-O.1-15-2", "kind": "list", "h": "d69dd89fdf", "text": "Exercise: \"Match the Gate to Its Meaning\", team race (Slide 16) Stories (not covered): Slides 18, 21"}], "html": "<div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"ddf39d3bff\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"O.1 §0 ¶0\" data-sec=\"0\" id=\"p-O.1-0-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference O.1 §0 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"O.1 §0 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§0 ¶0</button><h1 data-body=\"1\">Overview — The Divine Plumbline in Five Days</h1></div><div class=\"blk blk-paragraph\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"dbfdad167e\" data-kind=\"paragraph\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"O.1 §0 ¶1\" data-sec=\"0\" id=\"p-O.1-0-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference O.1 §0 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"O.1 §0 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§0 ¶1</button><p data-body=\"1\">A short summary of the five-day Divine Plumbline week, drawn from the plan (P.1) and the day texts (D.1–D.5). Each day has two parts of about 1.5 hours: Part 1 in the morning, Part 2 in the afternoon. Personal story slides are listed, not summarized; \"not covered\" means the story is still a placeholder.</p></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"faf4ce7100\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"O.1 §1 ¶0\" data-sec=\"1\" id=\"p-O.1-1-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference O.1 §1 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"O.1 §1 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§1 ¶0</button><h2 data-body=\"1\">Day 1 — Welcome &amp; The Plumbline / Truth and Deception</h2></div><div class=\"blk blk-paragraph\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"6407d1af46\" data-kind=\"paragraph\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"O.1 §1 ¶1\" data-sec=\"1\" id=\"p-O.1-1-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference O.1 §1 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"O.1 §1 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§1 ¶1</button><p data-body=\"1\"><a data-href=\"d1-day1.md\" data-internal=\"1\" href=\"#d-D.1\">Read Day 1 in full</a></p></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"71ea33c5f0\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"O.1 §2 ¶0\" data-sec=\"2\" id=\"p-O.1-2-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference O.1 §2 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"O.1 §2 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§2 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Part 1 — Welcome &amp; The Plumbline</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-paragraph\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"d7e991db99\" data-kind=\"paragraph\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"O.1 §2 ¶1\" data-sec=\"2\" id=\"p-O.1-2-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference O.1 §2 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"O.1 §2 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§2 ¶1</button><p data-body=\"1\">The week opens in prayer, with an honest, relational tone. Amos 7:7-9 introduces the plumbline: an absolute, unmoving standard. It is our behavior, not our personality, that is built like a wall, brick by protective brick; repeated long enough, it becomes a spiritual stronghold (2 Corinthians 10:4-5). The true plumbline to measure against is Christ. Then the four identity questions (Ontology, Epistemology, Teleology, Axiology), the Belief Tree, a world views survey, and the biblical \"heart\" as the whole inner person (Proverbs 4:23).</p></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"6ae1bb4523\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"2\" data-ref=\"O.1 §2 ¶2\" data-sec=\"2\" id=\"p-O.1-2-2\"><button aria-label=\"Reference O.1 §2 ¶2\" class=\"ref\" title=\"O.1 §2 ¶2 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§2 ¶2</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li><strong>Exercise:</strong> Icebreaker \"Growing Up\" (Slide 5)</li>\n<li><strong>Stories:</strong> Slides 1, 8 written (Review 1); personal, not summarized</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"12af2153a5\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"O.1 §3 ¶0\" data-sec=\"3\" id=\"p-O.1-3-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference O.1 §3 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"O.1 §3 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§3 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Part 2 — Truth and Deception</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-paragraph\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"d878eff20e\" data-kind=\"paragraph\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"O.1 §3 ¶1\" data-sec=\"3\" id=\"p-O.1-3-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference O.1 §3 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"O.1 §3 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§3 ¶1</button><p data-body=\"1\">Who has been telling us what to see: the seven false prophets (Parents, Teachers, Peers, Government, Media, Church, the Heart itself). None is evil by definition, but each becomes a false prophet when it replaces God's voice on who we are. Satan as the ultimate deceiver (Amos 5:19) plays the long game of distorted identity. Storms of life do not create the wall; they reveal it. Closes with the Day 1 recap and questions.</p></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"2d50e28b0f\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"2\" data-ref=\"O.1 §3 ¶2\" data-sec=\"3\" id=\"p-O.1-3-2\"><button aria-label=\"Reference O.1 §3 ¶2\" class=\"ref\" title=\"O.1 §3 ¶2 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§3 ¶2</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li><strong>Exercise:</strong> \"Which Storm Revealed Your Wall?\", quiet journaling (Slide 15)</li>\n<li><strong>Stories (not covered):</strong> Slides 12, 13</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"6238fbf296\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"O.1 §4 ¶0\" data-sec=\"4\" id=\"p-O.1-4-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference O.1 §4 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"O.1 §4 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§4 ¶0</button><h2 data-body=\"1\">Day 2 — Love Deficit &amp; the Generational Pattern / The Two Walls and the Four Situations</h2></div><div class=\"blk blk-paragraph\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"178a1ad445\" data-kind=\"paragraph\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"O.1 §4 ¶1\" data-sec=\"4\" id=\"p-O.1-4-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference O.1 §4 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"O.1 §4 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§4 ¶1</button><p data-body=\"1\"><a data-href=\"d2-day2.md\" data-internal=\"1\" href=\"#d-D.2\">Read Day 2 in full</a></p></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"f7e69995f5\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"O.1 §5 ¶0\" data-sec=\"5\" id=\"p-O.1-5-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference O.1 §5 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"O.1 §5 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§5 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Part 1 — Love Deficit and the Generational Pattern</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-paragraph\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"02f6cdfca1\" data-kind=\"paragraph\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"O.1 §5 ¶1\" data-sec=\"5\" id=\"p-O.1-5-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference O.1 §5 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"O.1 §5 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§5 ¶1</button><p data-body=\"1\">When the need to be loved, valued and secure is not met, an identity crisis begins. The Love Deficit equation; visiting vs. blessing (Exodus 20:5-6), with sour grapes as its picture (Ezekiel 18:2); and the generational symptom chain from the book, where childhood reactions harden into adult symptoms. The same deficit leads down two roads: Rejection (\"I am not enough\") and Rebellion (\"I will make myself enough\"). Through Jesus' atonement we can break our agreement with what was spoken over us.</p></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"5b3e609a45\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"2\" data-ref=\"O.1 §5 ¶2\" data-sec=\"5\" id=\"p-O.1-5-2\"><button aria-label=\"Reference O.1 §5 ¶2\" class=\"ref\" title=\"O.1 §5 ¶2 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§5 ¶2</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li><strong>Exercise:</strong> \"Where Do You Feel the Deficit?\", move to a corner of the room (Slide 5)</li>\n<li><strong>Stories (not covered):</strong> Slides 3, 7</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"49d576f8b0\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"O.1 §6 ¶0\" data-sec=\"6\" id=\"p-O.1-6-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference O.1 §6 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"O.1 §6 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§6 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Part 2 — The Two Walls and the Four Situations</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-paragraph\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"295b693fa1\" data-kind=\"paragraph\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"O.1 §6 ¶1\" data-sec=\"6\" id=\"p-O.1-6-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference O.1 §6 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"O.1 §6 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§6 ¶1</button><p data-body=\"1\">The Wall of Rejection (cornerstone: unbelief) and the Wall of Rebellion (cornerstone: pride), each with its brick inventory by Emotions, Intellect and Spirit. Everyone takes the Divine Plumbline Questionnaire (24 items, True/False) and scores it before the reveal. Then the four typical situations and their behaviors: O Compliant (Saul), U Can't Do It (Moses), W Competitive (Jacob), C Critical (Judas). They are not mutually exclusive: a high score names a wall you built, not who you are.</p></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"20f2fee237\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"2\" data-ref=\"O.1 §6 ¶2\" data-sec=\"6\" id=\"p-O.1-6-2\"><button aria-label=\"Reference O.1 §6 ¶2\" class=\"ref\" title=\"O.1 §6 ¶2 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§6 ¶2</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li><strong>Exercise:</strong> \"Both Walls\" diagram walkthrough &amp; scoring (Slide 17)</li>\n<li><strong>Stories (not covered):</strong> Slide 15</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"b2eea0ac02\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"O.1 §7 ¶0\" data-sec=\"7\" id=\"p-O.1-7-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference O.1 §7 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"O.1 §7 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§7 ¶0</button><h2 data-body=\"1\">Day 3 — The Lust Trap (Flight Swing) / The Pride Trap (Fight Swing) and the Final Swing</h2></div><div class=\"blk blk-paragraph\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"d589f5365f\" data-kind=\"paragraph\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"O.1 §7 ¶1\" data-sec=\"7\" id=\"p-O.1-7-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference O.1 §7 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"O.1 §7 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§7 ¶1</button><p data-body=\"1\"><a data-href=\"d3-day3.md\" data-internal=\"1\" href=\"#d-D.3\">Read Day 3 in full</a></p></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"d432f953e8\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"O.1 §8 ¶0\" data-sec=\"8\" id=\"p-O.1-8-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference O.1 §8 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"O.1 §8 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§8 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Part 1 — The Lust Trap (Flight Swing)</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-paragraph\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"1d2ce0f939\" data-kind=\"paragraph\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"O.1 §8 ¶1\" data-sec=\"8\" id=\"p-O.1-8-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference O.1 §8 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"O.1 §8 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§8 ¶1</button><p data-body=\"1\">Behind the Wall of Rejection: \"lust\" in a broad sense, any craving used to numb or escape pain, which deepens the wound it tries to escape. The Flight Swing moves between numbing/indulgence and self-punishment/withdrawal. Eating disorders serve as a case study (pastoral tone), and Solomon's Opponent-Process Theory explains why the swing grows more extreme.</p></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"ba7eac7563\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"2\" data-ref=\"O.1 §8 ¶2\" data-sec=\"8\" id=\"p-O.1-8-2\"><button aria-label=\"Reference O.1 §8 ¶2\" class=\"ref\" title=\"O.1 §8 ¶2 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§8 ¶2</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li><strong>Exercise:</strong> \"Naming the Swing\", quiet reflection and stretch (Slide 5)</li>\n<li><strong>Stories (not covered):</strong> Slide 4</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"84b1f7def0\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"O.1 §9 ¶0\" data-sec=\"9\" id=\"p-O.1-9-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference O.1 §9 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"O.1 §9 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§9 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Part 2 — The Pride Trap (Fight Swing) and the Final Swing</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-paragraph\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"5aef1ed6c7\" data-kind=\"paragraph\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"O.1 §9 ¶1\" data-sec=\"9\" id=\"p-O.1-9-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference O.1 §9 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"O.1 §9 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§9 ¶1</button><p data-body=\"1\">Behind the Wall of Rebellion: pride as self-sufficiency. The Fight Swing moves between control/domination and collapse/burnout, and secular humanism's \"Superman Philosophy\" can quietly reinforce it. The Final Swing: left unaddressed, both swings can converge toward the same destructive end, a kind of double-mindedness (a warning, not a diagnosis). Awareness is not enough; restoration is needed.</p></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"8c267bab8a\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"2\" data-ref=\"O.1 §9 ¶2\" data-sec=\"9\" id=\"p-O.1-9-2\"><button aria-label=\"Reference O.1 §9 ¶2\" class=\"ref\" title=\"O.1 §9 ¶2 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§9 ¶2</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li><strong>Exercise:</strong> \"Spot the Superman Message\", group activity (Slide 15)</li>\n<li><strong>Stories (not covered):</strong> Slide 12</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"75d60cb427\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"O.1 §10 ¶0\" data-sec=\"10\" id=\"p-O.1-10-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference O.1 §10 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"O.1 §10 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§10 ¶0</button><h2 data-body=\"1\">Day 4 — Restoration Begins: Revelation and Repentance / Release, Recognize, and Renounce</h2></div><div class=\"blk blk-paragraph\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"e0d9f1b130\" data-kind=\"paragraph\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"O.1 §10 ¶1\" data-sec=\"10\" id=\"p-O.1-10-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference O.1 §10 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"O.1 §10 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§10 ¶1</button><p data-body=\"1\"><a data-href=\"d4-day4.md\" data-internal=\"1\" href=\"#d-D.4\">Read Day 4 in full</a></p></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"6716185dce\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"O.1 §11 ¶0\" data-sec=\"11\" id=\"p-O.1-11-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference O.1 §11 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"O.1 §11 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§11 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Part 1 — Restoration Begins: Revelation and Repentance</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-paragraph\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"9324306743\" data-kind=\"paragraph\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"O.1 §11 ¶1\" data-sec=\"11\" id=\"p-O.1-11-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference O.1 §11 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"O.1 §11 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§11 ¶1</button><p data-body=\"1\">The tone shifts from diagnosis to restoration: the Wall of Salvation. First the altar and the temple (worship before structural repair), then two images of how God works: gold refined by fire and clay shaped by the Potter. He doesn't leave us to ourselves. An overview of the seven reinforcing rods, then Rod 1, Revelation (exposing to heal, not to shame), and Rod 2, Repentance (turning from unbelief or from pride), closing with a short written repentance sentence.</p></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"e33933b723\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"2\" data-ref=\"O.1 §11 ¶2\" data-sec=\"11\" id=\"p-O.1-11-2\"><button aria-label=\"Reference O.1 §11 ¶2\" class=\"ref\" title=\"O.1 §11 ¶2 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§11 ¶2</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li><strong>Exercise:</strong> \"Naming One Brick\", private (Slide 6)</li>\n<li><strong>Stories (not covered):</strong> Slide 5</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"e2628a9153\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"O.1 §12 ¶0\" data-sec=\"12\" id=\"p-O.1-12-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference O.1 §12 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"O.1 §12 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§12 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Part 2 — Release, Recognize, and Renounce</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-paragraph\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"9bc3977c03\" data-kind=\"paragraph\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"O.1 §12 ¶1\" data-sec=\"12\" id=\"p-O.1-12-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference O.1 §12 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"O.1 §12 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§12 ¶1</button><p data-body=\"1\">Rod 3, Release: forgiveness as releasing a debt, a decision rather than a feeling, with a private list of names as preparation for Personal Ministry Time. Rods 4–5: recognizing the enemy's tactics and renouncing them with an active, spoken break, practiced first as a group with a light example. Rods 1–5 are tied together, and the session sets up Personal Ministry Time.</p></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"ea735973ce\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"2\" data-ref=\"O.1 §12 ¶2\" data-sec=\"12\" id=\"p-O.1-12-2\"><button aria-label=\"Reference O.1 §12 ¶2\" class=\"ref\" title=\"O.1 §12 ¶2 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§12 ¶2</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li><strong>Exercise:</strong> Rods 4–5 Recognize and Renounce, stand-and-move (Slide 15)</li>\n<li><strong>Stories (not covered):</strong> Slide 14</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"f4903a355e\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"O.1 §13 ¶0\" data-sec=\"13\" id=\"p-O.1-13-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference O.1 §13 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"O.1 §13 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§13 ¶0</button><h2 data-body=\"1\">Day 5 — Renewing the Mind / Rebuilding, the Gates of Jerusalem, and Commissioning</h2></div><div class=\"blk blk-paragraph\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"cf25ef77db\" data-kind=\"paragraph\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"O.1 §13 ¶1\" data-sec=\"13\" id=\"p-O.1-13-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference O.1 §13 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"O.1 §13 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§13 ¶1</button><p data-body=\"1\"><a data-href=\"d5-day5.md\" data-internal=\"1\" href=\"#d-D.5\">Read Day 5 in full</a></p></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"bd37fd4cba\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"O.1 §14 ¶0\" data-sec=\"14\" id=\"p-O.1-14-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference O.1 §14 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"O.1 §14 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§14 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Part 1 — Renewing the Mind</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-paragraph\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"07f1826b85\" data-kind=\"paragraph\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"O.1 §14 ¶1\" data-sec=\"14\" id=\"p-O.1-14-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference O.1 §14 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"O.1 §14 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§14 ¶1</button><p data-body=\"1\">Rod 6, Renewing (Romans 12:2): an ongoing replacement of old thought patterns with truth, in eight steps: Acknowledging, Believing, Confessing, Desiring, Exercising (the A-C-D-B model), Forgiving, Growing, Helping. Includes writing one \"confession\" statement that replaces an old belief with a biblical truth.</p></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"7f657bf90f\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"2\" data-ref=\"O.1 §14 ¶2\" data-sec=\"14\" id=\"p-O.1-14-2\"><button aria-label=\"Reference O.1 §14 ¶2\" class=\"ref\" title=\"O.1 §14 ¶2 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§14 ¶2</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li><strong>Exercise:</strong> \"Practice the A-C-D-B Loop\", together and then privately (Slide 7)</li>\n<li><strong>Stories (not covered):</strong> Slides 4, 9</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"175a8b5204\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"O.1 §15 ¶0\" data-sec=\"15\" id=\"p-O.1-15-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference O.1 §15 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"O.1 §15 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§15 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Part 2 — Rebuilding, the Gates of Jerusalem, and Commissioning</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-paragraph\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"e05d037dad\" data-kind=\"paragraph\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"O.1 §15 ¶1\" data-sec=\"15\" id=\"p-O.1-15-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference O.1 §15 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"O.1 §15 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§15 ¶1</button><p data-body=\"1\">Rod 7, Rebuilding: a new wall laid on the true plumbline (Christ), following Nehemiah. The ten gates of Jerusalem as an allegory of restored life (Sheep, Fish, Old, Valley, Rubbish, Fountain, Water, Horse, East, Watch), and the King of Glory (Psalm 24), with a quiet look back at the Day 2 questionnaire results. The week closes with a full recap, testimonies and encouragement, and a commissioning prayer.</p></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"d69dd89fdf\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"2\" data-ref=\"O.1 §15 ¶2\" data-sec=\"15\" id=\"p-O.1-15-2\"><button aria-label=\"Reference O.1 §15 ¶2\" class=\"ref\" title=\"O.1 §15 ¶2 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§15 ¶2</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li><strong>Exercise:</strong> \"Match the Gate to Its Meaning\", team race (Slide 16)</li>\n<li><strong>Stories (not covered):</strong> Slides 18, 21</li>\n</ul></div>"}, {"id": "P.1", "title": "The Divine Plumbline — 5-Day DTS Program", "order": 1, "metadata": {"source": "src/divine-plumbline-5day-program.md", "kind": "5-day plan"}, "file": "p1-5day-program.md", "notes": ["<p>original (Q2, 2026-11-16): \"- Amos 7:7–9 — the plumbline vision; wall = personality\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Q3, 2026-11-16): \"## DAY 2 — The Two Walls and the Four Profiles\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Q3, 2026-11-16): \"- <strong>Administer the questionnaire</strong> (24-item T/F instrument) before revealing the four profiles, so scores aren't anchored by the teaching\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Q3, 2026-11-16): \"- <strong>Reveal: the Four Personality Profiles</strong>\"</p>\n"], "blocks": [{"ref": "P.1 §0 ¶0", "anchor": "p-P.1-0-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "55cab92ba3", "text": "The Divine Plumbline — 5-Day DTS Program"}, {"ref": "P.1 §0 ¶1", "anchor": "p-P.1-0-1", "kind": "paragraph", "h": "6ec937a674", "text": "Assumed standard DTS daily rhythm: worship/devotions → Lecture Block 1 → break → Lecture Block 2 → lunch → afternoon Personal Ministry Time (PMT) / homework / journaling. Adjust slot labels to your base's actual clock times."}, {"ref": "P.1 §1 ¶0", "anchor": "p-P.1-1-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "55ab2902b1", "text": "DAY 1 — Foundations: Identity and Deception"}, {"ref": "P.1 §1 ¶1", "anchor": "p-P.1-1-1", "kind": "list", "h": "472957300e", "text": "Lecture 1: The Divine Plumbline Amos 7:7–9 — the plumbline vision; wall = behavior (people may view it as personality) Four identity questions: Ontology, Epistemology, Teleology, Axiology Survey of world views (why all but Monotheism drift from \"I AM\") \"Heart\" defined biblically = whole inner person (soul + spirit) Lecture 2: Truth and Deception The seven \"false prophets\": Parents, Teachers, Peers, Government, Media, Church, the Heart itself Satan as ultimate deceiver — Amos 5:19 lion/bear/snake illustration Storms (Ezekiel 13:9–16) and the two ways a wall falls: clenched fist vs. broken/contrite heart PMT / Journal prompt: Which false prophets spoke into your own life? Recall one storm that exposed a wall."}, {"ref": "P.1 §2 ¶0", "anchor": "p-P.1-2-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "598e070ebd", "text": "DAY 2 — The Two Walls and the Four Situations"}, {"ref": "P.1 §2 ¶1", "anchor": "p-P.1-2-1", "kind": "list", "h": "f67afe6794", "text": "Lecture 1: The Love Deficit and Sour Grapes Love-deficit equations (You − Love = 0; You + Lust = 0; You + Love = Joy/Peace) Sour-grapes symptom chain (rage→crime, denial→personality disorders, etc.) Three response patterns: passive, aggressive, passive/aggressive Lecture 2: Rejection and Rebellion — the full brick lists Wall of Rejection bricks (Emotions/Intellect/Spirit) → suicide Wall of Rebellion bricks (Emotions/Intellect/Spirit) → homicide Cornerstones: unbelief (rejection) / pride (rebellion) Administer the questionnaire (24-item T/F instrument) before revealing the four situations, so scores aren't anchored by the teaching Reveal: the Four Typical Situations Compliant (Saul) / Can't Do It (Moses) — from Rejection Competitive (Jacob) / Critical (Judas) — from Rebellion Each with core message, strengths, weaknesses, manipulator-type mapping PMT / Journal prompt: Score your own questionnaire; mark every brick you personally identify with."}, {"ref": "P.1 §3 ¶0", "anchor": "p-P.1-3-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "0e801b9896", "text": "DAY 3 — Behind the Walls"}, {"ref": "P.1 §3 ¶1", "anchor": "p-P.1-3-1", "kind": "list", "h": "6c56e381f2", "text": "Lecture 1: The Lust Trap (Flight Swing) Pain → pleasure → temporary relief → return to pain (the addictive cycle) Eating disorders and their common parental preconditions Solomon's Opponent-Process Theory (pleasure always incurs a slower opposing pain) Lecture 2: The Pride Trap (Fight Swing) and the Final Swing Inferiority → fight for superiority; secular humanism's \"superman philosophy\" Emotions/Mind/Will soul-swings converging toward instability — \"double-mindedness\" (James 1:6–8) PMT / Small groups: Using your questionnaire result, identify whether your own pattern leans more toward flight (lust) or fight (pride) — discuss with a partner, not the whole group."}, {"ref": "P.1 §4 ¶0", "anchor": "p-P.1-4-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "093e626cf8", "text": "DAY 4 — Restoration Begins"}, {"ref": "P.1 §4 ¶1", "anchor": "p-P.1-4-1", "kind": "list", "h": "e3a7267839", "text": "Lecture 1: Foundations, Revelation, Repentance The Altar (confession/repentance/forgiveness) and the Temple (worship/intimacy) as the two foundations rebuilt first (Ezra 3) Rod 1 — Revelation: knowing Father God rightly vs. through a distorted human-father lens Rod 2 — Repentance: unbelief under Rejection, pride under Rebellion; humility as pride's antidote Lecture 2: Release, Recognize, Renounce Rod 3 — Release/Forgiveness: the unforgiving-servant parable; unforgiveness as \"tormentor\" Rods 4–5 — Recognizing Satan's tactics (anger/fear, mocking, confusion, false friends, demonic influence) and renouncing them; the four weapons (name/blood of Jesus, testimony, armor of God) PMT / Ministry time: Personal prayer ministry offered — walking a student through forgiveness release for one specific offense, using the Day 2 brick list as a starting point."}, {"ref": "P.1 §5 ¶0", "anchor": "p-P.1-5-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "481df3a082", "text": "DAY 5 — Renewing, Rebuilding, Commissioning"}, {"ref": "P.1 §5 ¶1", "anchor": "p-P.1-5-1", "kind": "list", "h": "ab33e23606", "text": "Lecture 1: Renewing — the eight steps Acknowledging → Believing → Confessing → Desiring → Exercising (A-C-D-B model) → Forgiving → Growing → Helping Practical exercise: walk through the eight steps against one identified wound from the week Lecture 2: Rebuilding and the Gates The nine/ten gates of Jerusalem allegorized (Sheep, Fish, Old, Valley, Rubbish, Fountain, Water, Horse, East, Watch) Closing image: the King of Glory's character (Galatians 5:22–23 fruit) as the \"different character\" that emerges Closing session: testimonies, re-score or reflect on the questionnaire in light of the week, commissioning/prayer over each student"}, {"ref": "P.1 §6 ¶0", "anchor": "p-P.1-6-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "021e96526b", "text": "Notes on pacing"}, {"ref": "P.1 §6 ¶1", "anchor": "p-P.1-6-1", "kind": "list", "h": "31aadd7e98", "text": "This compresses the source material's 8 study-guide sessions (15 book chapters) into 5 teaching days by pairing sessions 1–2, 3–4, and 5–6 into single days and giving 7–8 (the longest, most practical restoration content) two full days. If your base's DTS runs a half-day-per-lecture-block format rather than two blocks per day, split each day above into two, giving this 8–10 days instead of 5 — the content maps cleanly either way. Questionnaire timing (Day 2, before the reveal) is deliberate so students self-report before knowing what the letters mean — reconsider if your base prefers to teach the framework first and self-assess after."}], "html": "<div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"55cab92ba3\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"P.1 §0 ¶0\" data-sec=\"0\" id=\"p-P.1-0-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference P.1 §0 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"P.1 §0 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§0 ¶0</button><h1 data-body=\"1\">The Divine Plumbline — 5-Day DTS Program</h1></div><div class=\"blk blk-paragraph\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"6ec937a674\" data-kind=\"paragraph\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"P.1 §0 ¶1\" data-sec=\"0\" id=\"p-P.1-0-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference P.1 §0 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"P.1 §0 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§0 ¶1</button><p data-body=\"1\">Assumed standard DTS daily rhythm: worship/devotions → Lecture Block 1 → break → Lecture Block 2 → lunch → afternoon Personal Ministry Time (PMT) / homework / journaling. Adjust slot labels to your base's actual clock times.</p></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"55ab2902b1\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"P.1 §1 ¶0\" data-sec=\"1\" id=\"p-P.1-1-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference P.1 §1 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"P.1 §1 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§1 ¶0</button><h2 data-body=\"1\">DAY 1 — Foundations: Identity and Deception</h2></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"472957300e\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"P.1 §1 ¶1\" data-sec=\"1\" id=\"p-P.1-1-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference P.1 §1 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"P.1 §1 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§1 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li><strong>Lecture 1: The Divine Plumbline</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Amos 7:7–9 — the plumbline vision; wall = behavior (people may view it as personality)</li>\n<li>Four identity questions: Ontology, Epistemology, Teleology, Axiology</li>\n<li>Survey of world views (why all but Monotheism drift from \"I AM\")</li>\n<li>\"Heart\" defined biblically = whole inner person (soul + spirit)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Lecture 2: Truth and Deception</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>The seven \"false prophets\": Parents, Teachers, Peers, Government, Media, Church, the Heart itself</li>\n<li>Satan as ultimate deceiver — Amos 5:19 lion/bear/snake illustration</li>\n<li>Storms (Ezekiel 13:9–16) and the two ways a wall falls: clenched fist vs. broken/contrite heart</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>PMT / Journal prompt:</strong> Which false prophets spoke into your own life? Recall one storm that exposed a wall.</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"598e070ebd\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"P.1 §2 ¶0\" data-sec=\"2\" id=\"p-P.1-2-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference P.1 §2 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"P.1 §2 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§2 ¶0</button><h2 data-body=\"1\">DAY 2 — The Two Walls and the Four Situations</h2></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"f67afe6794\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"P.1 §2 ¶1\" data-sec=\"2\" id=\"p-P.1-2-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference P.1 §2 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"P.1 §2 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§2 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li><strong>Lecture 1: The Love Deficit and Sour Grapes</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Love-deficit equations (You − Love = 0; You + Lust = 0; You + Love = Joy/Peace)</li>\n<li>Sour-grapes symptom chain (rage→crime, denial→personality disorders, etc.)</li>\n<li>Three response patterns: passive, aggressive, passive/aggressive</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Lecture 2: Rejection and Rebellion — the full brick lists</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Wall of Rejection bricks (Emotions/Intellect/Spirit) → suicide</li>\n<li>Wall of Rebellion bricks (Emotions/Intellect/Spirit) → homicide</li>\n<li>Cornerstones: unbelief (rejection) / pride (rebellion)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Administer the questionnaire</strong> (24-item T/F instrument) before revealing the four situations, so scores aren't anchored by the teaching</li>\n<li><strong>Reveal: the Four Typical Situations</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Compliant (Saul) / Can't Do It (Moses) — from Rejection</li>\n<li>Competitive (Jacob) / Critical (Judas) — from Rebellion</li>\n<li>Each with core message, strengths, weaknesses, manipulator-type mapping</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>PMT / Journal prompt:</strong> Score your own questionnaire; mark every brick you personally identify with.</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"0e801b9896\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"P.1 §3 ¶0\" data-sec=\"3\" id=\"p-P.1-3-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference P.1 §3 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"P.1 §3 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§3 ¶0</button><h2 data-body=\"1\">DAY 3 — Behind the Walls</h2></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"6c56e381f2\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"P.1 §3 ¶1\" data-sec=\"3\" id=\"p-P.1-3-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference P.1 §3 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"P.1 §3 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§3 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li><strong>Lecture 1: The Lust Trap (Flight Swing)</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Pain → pleasure → temporary relief → return to pain (the addictive cycle)</li>\n<li>Eating disorders and their common parental preconditions</li>\n<li>Solomon's Opponent-Process Theory (pleasure always incurs a slower opposing pain)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Lecture 2: The Pride Trap (Fight Swing) and the Final Swing</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Inferiority → fight for superiority; secular humanism's \"superman philosophy\"</li>\n<li>Emotions/Mind/Will soul-swings converging toward instability — \"double-mindedness\" (James 1:6–8)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>PMT / Small groups:</strong> Using your questionnaire result, identify whether your own pattern leans more toward flight (lust) or fight (pride) — discuss with a partner, not the whole group.</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"093e626cf8\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"P.1 §4 ¶0\" data-sec=\"4\" id=\"p-P.1-4-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference P.1 §4 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"P.1 §4 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§4 ¶0</button><h2 data-body=\"1\">DAY 4 — Restoration Begins</h2></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"e3a7267839\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"P.1 §4 ¶1\" data-sec=\"4\" id=\"p-P.1-4-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference P.1 §4 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"P.1 §4 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§4 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li><strong>Lecture 1: Foundations, Revelation, Repentance</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>The Altar (confession/repentance/forgiveness) and the Temple (worship/intimacy) as the two foundations rebuilt first (Ezra 3)</li>\n<li>Rod 1 — Revelation: knowing Father God rightly vs. through a distorted human-father lens</li>\n<li>Rod 2 — Repentance: unbelief under Rejection, pride under Rebellion; humility as pride's antidote</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Lecture 2: Release, Recognize, Renounce</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Rod 3 — Release/Forgiveness: the unforgiving-servant parable; unforgiveness as \"tormentor\"</li>\n<li>Rods 4–5 — Recognizing Satan's tactics (anger/fear, mocking, confusion, false friends, demonic influence) and renouncing them; the four weapons (name/blood of Jesus, testimony, armor of God)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>PMT / Ministry time:</strong> Personal prayer ministry offered — walking a student through forgiveness release for one specific offense, using the Day 2 brick list as a starting point.</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"481df3a082\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"P.1 §5 ¶0\" data-sec=\"5\" id=\"p-P.1-5-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference P.1 §5 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"P.1 §5 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§5 ¶0</button><h2 data-body=\"1\">DAY 5 — Renewing, Rebuilding, Commissioning</h2></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"ab33e23606\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"P.1 §5 ¶1\" data-sec=\"5\" id=\"p-P.1-5-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference P.1 §5 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"P.1 §5 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§5 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li><strong>Lecture 1: Renewing — the eight steps</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Acknowledging → Believing → Confessing → Desiring → Exercising (A-C-D-B model) → Forgiving → Growing → Helping</li>\n<li>Practical exercise: walk through the eight steps against one identified wound from the week</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Lecture 2: Rebuilding and the Gates</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>The nine/ten gates of Jerusalem allegorized (Sheep, Fish, Old, Valley, Rubbish, Fountain, Water, Horse, East, Watch)</li>\n<li>Closing image: the King of Glory's character (Galatians 5:22–23 fruit) as the \"different character\" that emerges</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Closing session:</strong> testimonies, re-score or reflect on the questionnaire in light of the week, commissioning/prayer over each student</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"021e96526b\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"P.1 §6 ¶0\" data-sec=\"6\" id=\"p-P.1-6-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference P.1 §6 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"P.1 §6 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§6 ¶0</button><h2 data-body=\"1\">Notes on pacing</h2></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"31aadd7e98\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"P.1 §6 ¶1\" data-sec=\"6\" id=\"p-P.1-6-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference P.1 §6 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"P.1 §6 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§6 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>This compresses the source material's 8 study-guide sessions (15 book chapters) into 5 teaching days by pairing sessions 1–2, 3–4, and 5–6 into single days and giving 7–8 (the longest, most practical restoration content) two full days.</li>\n<li>If your base's DTS runs a half-day-per-lecture-block format rather than two blocks per day, split each day above into two, giving this 8–10 days instead of 5 — the content maps cleanly either way.</li>\n<li>Questionnaire timing (Day 2, before the reveal) is deliberate so students self-report before knowing what the letters mean — reconsider if your base prefers to teach the framework first and self-assess after.</li>\n</ul></div>"}, {"id": "D.1", "title": "Day 1 — Welcome & The Plumbline / Truth and Deception", "order": 2, "metadata": {"source": "src/tdp-day1.md", "kind": "Day 1"}, "file": "d1-day1.md", "notes": ["<p>added (Review 1, 2026-11-17)</p>\n", "<p>original (Review 1, 2026-11-17): \"- [story about xxx — an early failure or vulnerable moment of your own, to signal \"I'm not up here as someone with it all figured out\"]\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Review 1, 2026-11-17): \"- Set relational tone: this week works best when people are honest, not performing\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Review 1, 2026-11-17): \"- This isn't therapy and it isn't a performance review — it's a framework for understanding yourself biblically\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Review 1, 2026-11-17): \"- Promise: practical, not just theoretical — tools you can use starting this week\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Review 1, 2026-11-17): \"- Same question asked of Dr. Bruce Thompson decades ago — sparked a lifetime study of walls and plumblines in Scripture\"</p>\n", "<p>added (Review 1, 2026-11-17)</p>\n", "<p>original (Review 1, 2026-11-17): \"- The plumbline: an absolute, vertical, unmoving standard — not adjusted to fit the wall\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Review 1, 2026-11-17): \"- But behavior shapes personality more than we'd like to admit: repeat a protective behavior long enough, and you become very much what you act like — the wall, left unexamined, starts to feel like \"just who I am.\" This is what Scripture calls a <strong>spiritual stronghold</strong> (2 Corinthians 10:4-5) — a fortified pattern that feels permanent, but isn't\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Review 1, 2026-11-17): \"- Belief Tree: these four questions sit at the base of a larger picture — worldview and belief form the roots, values and principles grow from them, and behavior (our bricks) is simply the fruit. Get the roots wrong, and the fruit follows\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Review 1, 2026-11-17): \"- [story about xxx — a moment where you realized a behavior you struggled with was really a heart issue, not just a willpower issue]\"</p>\n"], "blocks": [{"ref": "D.1 §0 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.1-0-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "faf4ce7100", "text": "Day 1 — Welcome & The Plumbline / Truth and Deception"}, {"ref": "D.1 §0 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.1-0-1", "kind": "paragraph", "h": "26e40b6426", "text": "Day overview: Morning session (Part 1) introduces the week and the plumbline metaphor through trust-building and the four identity questions. Afternoon session (Part 2) covers where the \"wall's blueprint\" comes from — the seven false prophets, Satan as deceiver, and the storms of life. This is the first day of the whole program: no previous day to recap."}, {"ref": "D.1 §1 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.1-1-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "1c7b961ba0", "text": "PART 1 — Welcome & The Plumbline"}, {"ref": "D.1 §1 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.1-1-1", "kind": "paragraph", "h": "09a28ef788", "text": "Pacing note (≈1.5 hrs): opens straight into content (no recap — nothing came before it). Midpoint activity to break and refocus at Slide 5. Closes with just a quick mention of what Part 2 covers — no recap needed yet, and questions are held for the break, not this session."}, {"ref": "D.1 §2 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.1-2-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "90995b05ad", "text": "Slide 1 — Welcome to the Week"}, {"ref": "D.1 §2 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.1-2-1", "kind": "list", "h": "06ee5dead9", "text": "Open in prayer Welcome and warm greeting; introduce yourself as the teacher, not just a lecturer Name the week's destination without giving it all away: \"by Friday, you'll understand why your heart reacts the way it does — and what to do about it\" This is both for you to help others, but first to help you. Story: my wife and I staffed a few DTSes where Bruce and Barbara Thompson taught this. The first time it was sooo obvious where I needed help. But also the last time, there were things that I needed help with. Even preparing this helps me surrender more. Set relational tone: this week works best when people are honest, not performing. If you put up a mask, you will get help for the mask. So let's pray God gives us an environment where we can trust each other. To even express if we have reservations or distrust."}, {"ref": "D.1 §3 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.1-3-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "638750d740", "text": "Slide 2 — Why This Week Matters"}, {"ref": "D.1 §3 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.1-3-1", "kind": "list", "h": "6d82be12f4", "text": "Short, honest framing: everyone in the room has built protective patterns over the years This isn't a therapy session and it isn't a performance review. It's a framework for understanding yourself biblically, and to help you work through the issues the Holy Spirit wants you to take up, together with Him Practical, not just theoretical. Tools you can use starting this week"}, {"ref": "D.1 §4 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.1-4-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "1628102de9", "text": "Slide 3 — What Do You See? (Amos 7:7-9)"}, {"ref": "D.1 §4 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.1-4-1", "kind": "list", "h": "eb791db2a1", "text": "Read the passage aloud: the Lord shows Amos a plumbline against a wall God's question to Amos: \"What do you see?\" Same question asked of Dr. Bruce Thompson decades ago. It sparked a lifetime study of walls and plumblines in Scripture. So: what do you see? We will get to that. The plumbline: an absolute, vertical, unmoving standard — not adjusted to fit the wall, but the wall is adjusted to it"}, {"ref": "D.1 §5 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.1-5-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "2020831c6d", "text": "Slide 4 — Behavior Is Built Like a Wall"}, {"ref": "D.1 §5 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.1-5-1", "kind": "list", "h": "a79ffa5fdc", "text": "Important distinction, stated early and clearly: it's not our personality that's built like a wall — it's our behavior . Each brick is a protective response we build (or copy from watching others model it) to keep ourselves safe, best we could, at the time it was laid Some bricks are laid by us, many are laid by others (parents, teachers, culture, pain) But behavior shapes personality more than we'd like to admit: repeat a protective behavior long enough, and you become very much what you act like — the wall, left unexamined, starts to feel like \"just who I am.\" This is what Scripture calls a spiritual stronghold (2 Corinthians 10:4-5) — a fortified pattern that feels permanent (but, spoiler alert: it isn't) A plumbline has two uses: it's held against a wall while it's being built , to keep each new brick straight — and it's used afterward , to measure whether the wall actually is. Measured honestly, a crooked brick can be identified and replaced This week: identifying your bricks, and finding the true plumbline (Christ) to measure against — before behavior hardens into identity"}, {"ref": "D.1 §6 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.1-6-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "5568ec4267", "text": "Slide 5 — [exercise] Icebreaker: \"Growing Up\" — MIDPOINT BREAK & REFOCUS"}, {"ref": "D.1 §6 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.1-6-1", "kind": "list", "h": "b22555c127", "text": "Positive-focused exercise — pair-share or small group, two simple questions from childhood: How did you keep warm on cold days growing up? What's one episode where you remember feeling loved? Warm, easy entry point — good memories, not wounds; a relational, story-sharing breather halfway through the session before the next, more conceptual stretch Debrief briefly as a large group: invite a couple of volunteers to share one answer each"}, {"ref": "D.1 §7 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.1-7-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "ec9a5b824f", "text": "Slide 6 — The Four Identity Questions"}, {"ref": "D.1 §7 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.1-7-1", "kind": "list", "h": "03d6e794ab", "text": "Ontology: Who am I? Epistemology: How do I know what I know? Teleology: Why am I here / what's my purpose? Axiology: What do I value? Every worldview — Christian or not — answers these four questions, whether consciously or not Belief Tree: these four questions sit at the base of a larger picture — worldview and belief form the soil and roots; values and principles grow from them; behavior (our bricks) is the fruit. Get the roots wrong, and the fruit follows"}, {"ref": "D.1 §8 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.1-8-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "4377408fd1", "text": "Slide 7 — World Views Survey"}, {"ref": "D.1 §8 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.1-8-1", "kind": "list", "h": "6d4db4572d", "text": "Quick contrast: how different worldviews (naturalism, humanism, biblical Christianity, etc.) answer the four questions differently Point: our answers to these four questions shape everything else — including how our behavior-wall gets built To-do for later today (and worth raising in your one-on-one, if you have one): of the four identity questions, which one do you least know your own answer to?"}, {"ref": "D.1 §9 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.1-9-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "426986aaaf", "text": "Slide 8 — What Is \"The Heart\"?"}, {"ref": "D.1 §9 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.1-9-1", "kind": "list", "h": "7d5ef91f4e", "text": "Biblical definition: not just emotions — the whole inner person (Hebrew leb/lebab , Greek kardia ) Includes mind, will, emotions, and spirit together \"Out of it flow the issues of life\" (Proverbs 4:23) — why we start here instead of with behavior Story: when Maria and I got married, we both brought our issues with us. One of mine was anger, which was when things were too much out of the spectrum of what I would expect. Yes, there were underlying reasons, such as my inability to express my thoughts, but my response was not good for my wife nor my children. I did not want them to walk around on eggshells around me. I am still learning and still taking this to God. He still is good, Maria is still working through this with me. But it starts with the realization and being honest."}, {"ref": "D.1 §10 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.1-10-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "5b71dc0594", "text": "Slide 9 — Closing: What's Coming in Part 2"}, {"ref": "D.1 §10 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.1-10-1", "kind": "list", "h": "c59000e084", "text": "Quick, light mention only — no recap needed, this is the first session of the week: \"after the break, we look at where these behavior-patterns actually come from — the voices that shape us before we even know it\" Explicitly note: hold any questions for the break — we'll pick up discussion after Word of encouragement Close in prayer"}, {"ref": "D.1 §11 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.1-11-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "2d3d6b4775", "text": "☕ SHORT BREAK"}, {"ref": "D.1 §12 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.1-12-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "6f12213113", "text": "PART 2 — Truth and Deception"}, {"ref": "D.1 §12 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.1-12-1", "kind": "paragraph", "h": "86a6a7ce88", "text": "Pacing note (≈1.5 hrs): opens with just a quick mention of what this session covers (and a chance to answer any plenum question that came up during the break) — no recap needed yet. Midpoint activity to break and refocus around Slide 15 (position 6 of 7 — after the halfway point, not before). Closes with the full Day 1 recap and time for questions."}, {"ref": "D.1 §13 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.1-13-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "17c3432426", "text": "Slide 10 — Opening: What's Next"}, {"ref": "D.1 §13 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.1-13-1", "kind": "list", "h": "362bc8ce8d", "text": "Open in prayer Quick, light mention only: \"this morning we asked what do you see — now let's ask who's been telling you what to see\" If a question came up in the break, take a moment to answer it in plenum here before moving on No recap of Part 1 needed — we're continuing the same day"}, {"ref": "D.1 §14 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.1-14-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "a3ef2c4abf", "text": "Slide 11 — The Seven False Prophets"}, {"ref": "D.1 §14 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.1-14-1", "kind": "list", "h": "b1cf435c28", "text": "Introduce the concept: voices that shape our sense of identity before we can evaluate them List the seven: Parents, Teachers, Peers, Government, Media, Church, the Heart itself Key point: none of these are evil by definition — but each can become a \"false prophet\" when it replaces God's voice as the final word on who we are"}, {"ref": "D.1 §15 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.1-15-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "a302d8435b", "text": "Slide 12 — The False Prophets, One by One"}, {"ref": "D.1 §15 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.1-15-1", "kind": "list", "h": "12598e5859", "text": "[story about xxx — a specific false prophet (parent, teacher, peer, etc.) that shaped your own self-image growing up, for better or worse] Parents, Teachers, Peers: a parent's unspoken disappointment, a teacher's offhand label, peer rejection or approval — ask the room (rhetorical, not forced sharing): which had the loudest voice in your life? Government, Media, the Church: identity defined by nation, class, achievement; manufactured images of worth, beauty, success; even the Church can become a false prophet when performance or legalism replaces grace as the measure of identity The Heart Itself: Jeremiah 17:9 — \"the heart is deceitful above all things.\" Our own self-talk and self-judgment can be the loudest false prophet of all — sets up the whole week's tension: we need an outside, unmoving plumbline, not our own shifting self-assessment"}, {"ref": "D.1 §16 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.1-16-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "4e21138cfd", "text": "Slide 13 — Satan as the Ultimate Deceiver"}, {"ref": "D.1 §16 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.1-16-1", "kind": "list", "h": "5eba9dc574", "text": "Amos 5:19 — the lion, the bear, the snake: escape one danger only to run into another Satan's strategy isn't usually a single dramatic attack — it's the long game of distorted identity Deception works best when it feels like our own conclusion, not an attack [story about xxx — a personal experience of recognizing a spiritual attack for what it was, and how you responded]"}, {"ref": "D.1 §17 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.1-17-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "3e75dbded1", "text": "Slide 14 — The Storms of Life"}, {"ref": "D.1 §17 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.1-17-1", "kind": "list", "h": "657685908e", "text": "Storms (crisis, loss, failure, betrayal) don't create the wall — they reveal it What's already built into the heart determines how we respond when the storm hits Sets up Day 2: the actual bricks that make up these walls"}, {"ref": "D.1 §18 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.1-18-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "cbf809e950", "text": "Slide 15 — [exercise] \"Which Storm Revealed Your Wall?\" — MIDPOINT BREAK & REFOCUS"}, {"ref": "D.1 §18 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.1-18-1", "kind": "list", "h": "01de1406ba", "text": "Optional quiet journaling prompt: think of a recent hard moment — what did your immediate, gut-level reaction reveal about what's already built inside? Not shared aloud unless someone volunteers — this is personal excavation, not a public exercise"}, {"ref": "D.1 §19 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.1-19-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "89c5e9c29d", "text": "Slide 16 — Recap: Day 1 in Review + Questions"}, {"ref": "D.1 §19 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.1-19-1", "kind": "list", "h": "971511f698", "text": "Full recap of the day's arc: the plumbline as an absolute standard → behavior built like a wall → the four identity questions → the heart as the whole inner person → the seven false prophets that mis-shape it → storms that reveal what's already built Open the floor for questions from today's teaching Bridge to tomorrow: \"tomorrow we get specific — the actual bricks, and the two walls most of us end up building\" Word of encouragement Close in prayer"}], "html": "<div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"faf4ce7100\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.1 §0 ¶0\" data-sec=\"0\" id=\"p-D.1-0-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.1 §0 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.1 §0 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§0 ¶0</button><h1 data-body=\"1\">Day 1 — Welcome &amp; The Plumbline / Truth and Deception</h1></div><div class=\"blk blk-paragraph\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"26e40b6426\" data-kind=\"paragraph\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.1 §0 ¶1\" data-sec=\"0\" id=\"p-D.1-0-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.1 §0 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.1 §0 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§0 ¶1</button><p data-body=\"1\"><strong>Day overview:</strong> Morning session (Part 1) introduces the week and the plumbline metaphor through trust-building and the four identity questions. Afternoon session (Part 2) covers where the \"wall's blueprint\" comes from — the seven false prophets, Satan as deceiver, and the storms of life. This is the first day of the whole program: no previous day to recap.</p></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"1c7b961ba0\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.1 §1 ¶0\" data-sec=\"1\" id=\"p-D.1-1-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.1 §1 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.1 §1 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§1 ¶0</button><h2 data-body=\"1\">PART 1 — Welcome &amp; The Plumbline</h2></div><div class=\"blk blk-paragraph\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"09a28ef788\" data-kind=\"paragraph\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.1 §1 ¶1\" data-sec=\"1\" id=\"p-D.1-1-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.1 §1 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.1 §1 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§1 ¶1</button><p data-body=\"1\"><strong>Pacing note (≈1.5 hrs):</strong> opens straight into content (no recap — nothing came before it). Midpoint activity to break and refocus at Slide 5. Closes with just a quick mention of what Part 2 covers — no recap needed yet, and questions are held for the break, not this session.</p></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"90995b05ad\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.1 §2 ¶0\" data-sec=\"2\" id=\"p-D.1-2-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.1 §2 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.1 §2 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§2 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 1 — Welcome to the Week</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"06ee5dead9\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.1 §2 ¶1\" data-sec=\"2\" id=\"p-D.1-2-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.1 §2 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.1 §2 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§2 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Open in prayer</li>\n<li>Welcome and warm greeting; introduce yourself as the teacher, not just a lecturer</li>\n<li>Name the week's destination without giving it all away: \"by Friday, you'll understand why your heart reacts the way it does — and what to do about it\"</li>\n<li>This is both for you to help others, but first to help you.</li>\n<li>Story: my wife and I staffed a few DTSes where Bruce and Barbara Thompson taught this. The first time it was sooo obvious where I needed help. But also the last time, there were things that I needed help with. Even preparing this helps me surrender more.</li>\n<li>Set relational tone: this week works best when people are honest, not performing. If you put up a mask, you will get help for the mask. So let's pray God gives us an environment where we can trust each other. To even express if we have reservations or distrust.</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"638750d740\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.1 §3 ¶0\" data-sec=\"3\" id=\"p-D.1-3-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.1 §3 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.1 §3 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§3 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 2 — Why This Week Matters</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"6d82be12f4\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.1 §3 ¶1\" data-sec=\"3\" id=\"p-D.1-3-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.1 §3 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.1 §3 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§3 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Short, honest framing: everyone in the room has built protective patterns over the years</li>\n<li>This isn't a therapy session and it isn't a performance review. It's a framework for understanding yourself biblically, and to help you work through the issues the Holy Spirit wants you to take up, together with Him</li>\n<li>Practical, not just theoretical. Tools you can use starting this week</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"1628102de9\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.1 §4 ¶0\" data-sec=\"4\" id=\"p-D.1-4-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.1 §4 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.1 §4 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§4 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 3 — What Do You See? (Amos 7:7-9)</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"eb791db2a1\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.1 §4 ¶1\" data-sec=\"4\" id=\"p-D.1-4-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.1 §4 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.1 §4 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§4 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Read the passage aloud: the Lord shows Amos a plumbline against a wall</li>\n<li>God's question to Amos: \"What do you see?\"</li>\n<li>Same question asked of Dr. Bruce Thompson decades ago. It sparked a lifetime study of walls and plumblines in Scripture.</li>\n<li>So: what do you see? We will get to that.</li>\n<li>The plumbline: an absolute, vertical, unmoving standard — not adjusted to fit the wall, but the wall is adjusted to it</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"2020831c6d\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.1 §5 ¶0\" data-sec=\"5\" id=\"p-D.1-5-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.1 §5 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.1 §5 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§5 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 4 — Behavior Is Built Like a Wall</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"a79ffa5fdc\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.1 §5 ¶1\" data-sec=\"5\" id=\"p-D.1-5-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.1 §5 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.1 §5 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§5 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Important distinction, stated early and clearly: it's not our <strong>personality</strong> that's built like a wall — it's our <strong>behavior</strong>. Each brick is a protective response we build (or copy from watching others model it) to keep ourselves safe, best we could, at the time it was laid</li>\n<li>Some bricks are laid by us, many are laid by others (parents, teachers, culture, pain)</li>\n<li>But behavior shapes personality more than we'd like to admit: repeat a protective behavior long enough, and you become very much what you act like — the wall, left unexamined, starts to feel like \"just who I am.\" This is what Scripture calls a <strong>spiritual stronghold</strong> (2 Corinthians 10:4-5) — a fortified pattern that feels permanent (but, spoiler alert: it isn't)</li>\n<li>A plumbline has two uses: it's held against a wall <em>while it's being built</em>, to keep each new brick straight — and it's used <em>afterward</em>, to measure whether the wall actually is. Measured honestly, a crooked brick can be identified and replaced</li>\n<li>This week: identifying your bricks, and finding the true plumbline (Christ) to measure against — before behavior hardens into identity</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"5568ec4267\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.1 §6 ¶0\" data-sec=\"6\" id=\"p-D.1-6-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.1 §6 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.1 §6 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§6 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 5 — <span class=\"ph-exercise\">[exercise]</span> Icebreaker: \"Growing Up\" — MIDPOINT BREAK &amp; REFOCUS</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"b22555c127\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.1 §6 ¶1\" data-sec=\"6\" id=\"p-D.1-6-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.1 §6 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.1 §6 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§6 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Positive-focused exercise — pair-share or small group, two simple questions from childhood:\n<ol>\n<li>How did you keep warm on cold days growing up?</li>\n<li>What's one episode where you remember feeling loved?</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li>Warm, easy entry point — good memories, not wounds; a relational, story-sharing breather halfway through the session before the next, more conceptual stretch</li>\n<li>Debrief briefly as a large group: invite a couple of volunteers to share one answer each</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"ec9a5b824f\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.1 §7 ¶0\" data-sec=\"7\" id=\"p-D.1-7-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.1 §7 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.1 §7 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§7 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 6 — The Four Identity Questions</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"03d6e794ab\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.1 §7 ¶1\" data-sec=\"7\" id=\"p-D.1-7-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.1 §7 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.1 §7 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§7 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Ontology: Who am I?</li>\n<li>Epistemology: How do I know what I know?</li>\n<li>Teleology: Why am I here / what's my purpose?</li>\n<li>Axiology: What do I value?</li>\n<li>Every worldview — Christian or not — answers these four questions, whether consciously or not</li>\n<li>Belief Tree: these four questions sit at the base of a larger picture — worldview and belief form the soil and roots; values and principles grow from them; behavior (our bricks) is the fruit. Get the roots wrong, and the fruit follows</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"4377408fd1\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.1 §8 ¶0\" data-sec=\"8\" id=\"p-D.1-8-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.1 §8 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.1 §8 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§8 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 7 — World Views Survey</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"6d4db4572d\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.1 §8 ¶1\" data-sec=\"8\" id=\"p-D.1-8-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.1 §8 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.1 §8 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§8 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Quick contrast: how different worldviews (naturalism, humanism, biblical Christianity, etc.) answer the four questions differently</li>\n<li>Point: our answers to these four questions shape everything else — including how our behavior-wall gets built</li>\n<li><strong>To-do for later today</strong> (and worth raising in your one-on-one, if you have one): of the four identity questions, which one do you least know your own answer to?</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"426986aaaf\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.1 §9 ¶0\" data-sec=\"9\" id=\"p-D.1-9-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.1 §9 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.1 §9 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§9 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 8 — What Is \"The Heart\"?</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"7d5ef91f4e\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.1 §9 ¶1\" data-sec=\"9\" id=\"p-D.1-9-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.1 §9 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.1 §9 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§9 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Biblical definition: not just emotions — the whole inner person (Hebrew <em>leb/lebab</em>, Greek <em>kardia</em>)</li>\n<li>Includes mind, will, emotions, and spirit together</li>\n<li>\"Out of it flow the issues of life\" (Proverbs 4:23) — why we start here instead of with behavior</li>\n<li>Story: when Maria and I got married, we both brought our issues with us. One of mine was anger, which was when things were too much out of the spectrum of what I would expect. Yes, there were underlying reasons, such as my inability to express my thoughts, but my response was not good for my wife nor my children. I did not want them to walk around on eggshells around me. I am still learning and still taking this to God. He still is good, Maria is still working through this with me. But it starts with the realization and being honest.</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"5b71dc0594\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.1 §10 ¶0\" data-sec=\"10\" id=\"p-D.1-10-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.1 §10 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.1 §10 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§10 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 9 — Closing: What's Coming in Part 2</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"c59000e084\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.1 §10 ¶1\" data-sec=\"10\" id=\"p-D.1-10-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.1 §10 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.1 §10 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§10 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Quick, light mention only — no recap needed, this is the first session of the week: \"after the break, we look at where these behavior-patterns actually come from — the voices that shape us before we even know it\"</li>\n<li>Explicitly note: hold any questions for the break — we'll pick up discussion after</li>\n<li>Word of encouragement</li>\n<li>Close in prayer</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"2d3d6b4775\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.1 §11 ¶0\" data-sec=\"11\" id=\"p-D.1-11-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.1 §11 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.1 §11 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§11 ¶0</button><h2 data-body=\"1\">☕ SHORT BREAK</h2></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"6f12213113\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.1 §12 ¶0\" data-sec=\"12\" id=\"p-D.1-12-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.1 §12 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.1 §12 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§12 ¶0</button><h2 data-body=\"1\">PART 2 — Truth and Deception</h2></div><div class=\"blk blk-paragraph\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"86a6a7ce88\" data-kind=\"paragraph\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.1 §12 ¶1\" data-sec=\"12\" id=\"p-D.1-12-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.1 §12 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.1 §12 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§12 ¶1</button><p data-body=\"1\"><strong>Pacing note (≈1.5 hrs):</strong> opens with just a quick mention of what this session covers (and a chance to answer any plenum question that came up during the break) — no recap needed yet. Midpoint activity to break and refocus around Slide 15 (position 6 of 7 — after the halfway point, not before). Closes with the full Day 1 recap and time for questions.</p></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"17c3432426\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.1 §13 ¶0\" data-sec=\"13\" id=\"p-D.1-13-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.1 §13 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.1 §13 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§13 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 10 — Opening: What's Next</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"362bc8ce8d\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.1 §13 ¶1\" data-sec=\"13\" id=\"p-D.1-13-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.1 §13 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.1 §13 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§13 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Open in prayer</li>\n<li>Quick, light mention only: \"this morning we asked what do you see — now let's ask who's been telling you what to see\"</li>\n<li>If a question came up in the break, take a moment to answer it in plenum here before moving on</li>\n<li>No recap of Part 1 needed — we're continuing the same day</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"a3ef2c4abf\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.1 §14 ¶0\" data-sec=\"14\" id=\"p-D.1-14-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.1 §14 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.1 §14 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§14 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 11 — The Seven False Prophets</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"b1cf435c28\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.1 §14 ¶1\" data-sec=\"14\" id=\"p-D.1-14-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.1 §14 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.1 §14 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§14 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Introduce the concept: voices that shape our sense of identity before we can evaluate them</li>\n<li>List the seven: Parents, Teachers, Peers, Government, Media, Church, the Heart itself</li>\n<li>Key point: none of these are evil by definition — but each can become a \"false prophet\" when it replaces God's voice as the final word on who we are</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"a302d8435b\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.1 §15 ¶0\" data-sec=\"15\" id=\"p-D.1-15-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.1 §15 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.1 §15 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§15 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 12 — The False Prophets, One by One</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"12598e5859\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.1 §15 ¶1\" data-sec=\"15\" id=\"p-D.1-15-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.1 §15 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.1 §15 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§15 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li><span class=\"ph-story\">[story about xxx — a specific false prophet (parent, teacher, peer, etc.) that shaped your own self-image growing up, for better or worse]</span></li>\n<li>Parents, Teachers, Peers: a parent's unspoken disappointment, a teacher's offhand label, peer rejection or approval — ask the room (rhetorical, not forced sharing): which had the loudest voice in your life?</li>\n<li>Government, Media, the Church: identity defined by nation, class, achievement; manufactured images of worth, beauty, success; even the Church can become a false prophet when performance or legalism replaces grace as the measure of identity</li>\n<li>The Heart Itself: Jeremiah 17:9 — \"the heart is deceitful above all things.\" Our own self-talk and self-judgment can be the loudest false prophet of all — sets up the whole week's tension: we need an outside, unmoving plumbline, not our own shifting self-assessment</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"4e21138cfd\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.1 §16 ¶0\" data-sec=\"16\" id=\"p-D.1-16-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.1 §16 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.1 §16 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§16 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 13 — Satan as the Ultimate Deceiver</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"5eba9dc574\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.1 §16 ¶1\" data-sec=\"16\" id=\"p-D.1-16-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.1 §16 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.1 §16 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§16 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Amos 5:19 — the lion, the bear, the snake: escape one danger only to run into another</li>\n<li>Satan's strategy isn't usually a single dramatic attack — it's the long game of distorted identity</li>\n<li>Deception works best when it feels like our own conclusion, not an attack</li>\n<li><span class=\"ph-story\">[story about xxx — a personal experience of recognizing a spiritual attack for what it was, and how you responded]</span></li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"3e75dbded1\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.1 §17 ¶0\" data-sec=\"17\" id=\"p-D.1-17-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.1 §17 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.1 §17 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§17 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 14 — The Storms of Life</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"657685908e\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.1 §17 ¶1\" data-sec=\"17\" id=\"p-D.1-17-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.1 §17 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.1 §17 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§17 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Storms (crisis, loss, failure, betrayal) don't create the wall — they reveal it</li>\n<li>What's already built into the heart determines how we respond when the storm hits</li>\n<li>Sets up Day 2: the actual bricks that make up these walls</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"cbf809e950\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.1 §18 ¶0\" data-sec=\"18\" id=\"p-D.1-18-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.1 §18 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.1 §18 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§18 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 15 — <span class=\"ph-exercise\">[exercise]</span> \"Which Storm Revealed Your Wall?\" — MIDPOINT BREAK &amp; REFOCUS</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"01de1406ba\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.1 §18 ¶1\" data-sec=\"18\" id=\"p-D.1-18-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.1 §18 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.1 §18 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§18 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Optional quiet journaling prompt: think of a recent hard moment — what did your immediate, gut-level reaction reveal about what's already built inside?</li>\n<li>Not shared aloud unless someone volunteers — this is personal excavation, not a public exercise</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"89c5e9c29d\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.1 §19 ¶0\" data-sec=\"19\" id=\"p-D.1-19-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.1 §19 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.1 §19 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§19 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 16 — Recap: Day 1 in Review + Questions</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"971511f698\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.1 §19 ¶1\" data-sec=\"19\" id=\"p-D.1-19-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.1 §19 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.1 §19 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§19 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Full recap of the day's arc: the plumbline as an absolute standard → behavior built like a wall → the four identity questions → the heart as the whole inner person → the seven false prophets that mis-shape it → storms that reveal what's already built</li>\n<li>Open the floor for questions from today's teaching</li>\n<li>Bridge to tomorrow: \"tomorrow we get specific — the actual bricks, and the two walls most of us end up building\"</li>\n<li>Word of encouragement</li>\n<li>Close in prayer</li>\n</ul></div>"}, {"id": "D.2", "title": "Day 2 — Love Deficit & the Generational Pattern / The Two Walls and the Four Situations", "order": 3, "metadata": {"source": "src/tdp-day2.md", "kind": "Day 2"}, "file": "d2-day2.md", "notes": ["<p>original title (Q3, 2026-11-16): \"Day 2 — Love Deficit &amp; the Generational Pattern / The Two Walls and the Four Structures\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Q3, 2026-11-16): \"<strong>Day overview:</strong> Morning session (Part 1) opens with a recap of Day 1, then covers the Love Deficit equations and the generational pattern (visiting vs. blessing, with sour grapes as its picture). Afternoon session (Part 2) covers the full brick inventory for both walls, administers the Divine Plumbline Questionnaire, then reveals the four typical structures and their behaviours.\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Q3, 2026-11-16): \"## PART 2 — The Two Walls and the Four Structures\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Q3, 2026-11-16): \"### Slide 18 — Reveal: Four Typical Structures and Their Behaviours\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Q3, 2026-11-16): \"- Not personality: these are four typical wall structures, and the behaviours each one produces\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Q3, 2026-11-16): \"### Slide 19 — The Four Structures in Depth\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Q3, 2026-11-16): \"- Full recap: Love Deficit → the generational pattern (visiting vs. blessing) → the two walls' brick inventories → your own questionnaire score → the four typical structures, their behaviours and their biblical archetypes\"</p>\n"], "blocks": [{"ref": "D.2 §0 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.2-0-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "6238fbf296", "text": "Day 2 — Love Deficit & the Generational Pattern / The Two Walls and the Four Situations"}, {"ref": "D.2 §0 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.2-0-1", "kind": "paragraph", "h": "2f2d1aa120", "text": "Day overview: Morning session (Part 1) opens with a recap of Day 1, then covers the Love Deficit equations and the generational pattern (visiting vs. blessing, with sour grapes as its picture). Afternoon session (Part 2) covers the full brick inventory for both walls, administers the Divine Plumbline Questionnaire, then reveals the four typical situations and their behaviors."}, {"ref": "D.2 §1 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.2-1-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "e0aa7cba5b", "text": "PART 1 — Love Deficit and the Generational Pattern"}, {"ref": "D.2 §1 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.2-1-1", "kind": "paragraph", "h": "dcdb25c0b6", "text": "Pacing note (≈1.5 hrs): opens with the Day 1 recap and takes any questions that came up during the day. One exercise this session, positioned at the halfway point (Slide 5 of 9) as a physical interruption to refocus — not earlier. Closes with just a quick mention of what Part 2 covers — no full recap yet, questions held for the break."}, {"ref": "D.2 §2 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.2-2-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "2aeec36b3e", "text": "Slide 1 — Yesterday, Quickly — and Any Questions"}, {"ref": "D.2 §2 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.2-2-1", "kind": "list", "h": "02a72c0303", "text": "Open in prayer One-slide reminder of Day 1: the plumbline as God's unmoving standard; behavior built like a wall; the seven false prophets; storms reveal what's already built Open the floor briefly for any questions that came up for people since yesterday Today's turn: \"now we look at exactly how the bricks get laid\""}, {"ref": "D.2 §3 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.2-3-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "4ff5506d9d", "text": "Slide 2 — Identity Crisis: Where It Starts"}, {"ref": "D.2 §3 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.2-3-1", "kind": "list", "h": "bafddf496b", "text": "Every human being is born with a need to be loved, valued, and secure When that need isn't met — or isn't met consistently — an identity crisis begins This isn't about blaming parents; it's about understanding the mechanism"}, {"ref": "D.2 §4 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.2-4-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "5a3f7e682e", "text": "Slide 3 — The Love Deficit Equation"}, {"ref": "D.2 §4 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.2-4-1", "kind": "list", "h": "a8de07393b", "text": "Present the equation(s) from the book: unmet love/value/security needs → deficit → compensating behavior Explain simply: every person, at some level, is either resting in being loved or working to earn/prove it [story about xxx — a time you caught yourself \"performing\" for love or approval rather than resting in being loved]"}, {"ref": "D.2 §5 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.2-5-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "9bdf5c833f", "text": "Slide 4 — The Principle: Visiting vs. Blessing (Exodus 20:5-6)"}, {"ref": "D.2 §5 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.2-5-1", "kind": "list", "h": "aaf5a16964", "text": "The law states the principle: God visits the iniquity of the fathers on the children, to the third and fourth generation (Exodus 20:5, 34:7; Deuteronomy 5:9) — this is where the concept of generational sin is rooted Unresolved pain and wrong patterns don't stay contained — they pass down But read the contrast in the same breath: steadfast love to thousands, to a thousand generations of those who love Him (Exodus 20:6; Deuteronomy 7:9). The blessing outreaches the visiting by far — that is what God is really saying The picture of how it passes down: sour grapes — \"The parents have eaten sour grapes, and the children's teeth are set on edge\" (Ezekiel 18:2, Jeremiah 31:29). People consuming and accepting what is wrong, and the next generation tasting it Not fatalism — naming the pattern is the first step to breaking it, and the greater weight is on the blessing side"}, {"ref": "D.2 §6 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.2-6-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "38278b886d", "text": "Slide 5 — [exercise] Where Do You Feel the Deficit? — MIDPOINT BREAK & REFOCUS"}, {"ref": "D.2 §6 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.2-6-1", "kind": "list", "h": "bccfd5a1cf", "text": "Quick physical/movement version: have people stand and move to a corner of the room representing where they feel most \"unfinished\" — being loved, being valued, or being secure Good stretch point at the halfway mark — no need to explain individual choices, just notice the room is genuinely split"}, {"ref": "D.2 §7 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.2-7-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "c8627d014e", "text": "Slide 6 — The Generational Symptom Chain"}, {"ref": "D.2 §7 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.2-7-1", "kind": "list", "h": "2ecb9988a1", "text": "Explain: the book cites U.S. military research on children whose fathers were away on deployment — the unresolved reactions of a child calcify over time into recognizable adult symptoms. The childhood reaction is the seed, the adult symptom is the fruit Show the table (childhood reaction → adult symptom): Rage → Crime Denial & Fantasy → Personality disorders Reunion attempts → Possessiveness Guilt → Depression (turned inward) / Delinquency (turned outward) Fear → Neuroses Impulse changes → Psychosomatic disorders Regression → Psychoses Example, worked through one line: a boy whose father is repeatedly absent feels guilt (\"it must be my fault\"). Turned inward, that guilt becomes depression; turned outward, it becomes acting out and delinquency. Same childhood reaction, two directions — which sets up the two walls Same pattern at everyday scale: a child who never gets the attention they need keeps trying to \"reunite\" — and as an adult clings to partners and friends (possessiveness) Key point: this is the mechanism of the generational pattern — unresolved reactions don't stay put, they harden and get passed on. At any link in the chain, truth can interrupt the cycle"}, {"ref": "D.2 §7 ¶2", "anchor": "p-D.2-7-2", "kind": "paragraph", "h": "966c97fa5d", "text": "Delivery note: the clinical terms (psychoses, personality disorders) are the book's research labels — present them as illustrations of how far unresolved reactions can travel, not as labels for anyone in the room."}, {"ref": "D.2 §8 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.2-8-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "f5df79a1b6", "text": "Slide 7 — [story about xxx — a generational pattern you noticed in your own family line, and a moment where it started to break]"}, {"ref": "D.2 §9 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.2-9-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "367353b302", "text": "Slide 8 — Two Roads From the Same Deficit"}, {"ref": "D.2 §9 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.2-9-1", "kind": "list", "h": "cc1e93a953", "text": "Preview: the same unmet need can produce two very different walls Rejection: withdrawing, believing \"I am not enough\" Rebellion: asserting, believing \"I will make myself enough\" Both are the same wound, answered in opposite directions — sets up the rest of the day We reinforce what those around us say: we want to belong, and we suffer from confirmation bias. First we think about what people say, then we accept it, agree with it, believe it — and finally speak it out over ourselves But this can be broken: Jesus is the atonement for our sins, and that is the root that lets you and me break our agreement with what was said — and not pay the price for it \"Anything you say can be held against you in a court of law\" — not if I recant it. Jesus has already paid for it, so the accusation cannot stand It gets replaced with what God says about us — every day, every time Sets up Days 4–5: a place to bring our daily struggles, and the words to break anything anyone has spoken over us"}, {"ref": "D.2 §9 ¶2", "anchor": "p-D.2-9-2", "kind": "paragraph", "h": "12f6d4dc83", "text": "Delivery note: the courtroom line works best with a light touch and a smile (\"Tell Jesus the charge still stands!\") — humor makes the truth land, not lighter."}, {"ref": "D.2 §10 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.2-10-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "d561b753cc", "text": "Slide 9 — What's Coming in Part 2"}, {"ref": "D.2 §10 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.2-10-1", "kind": "list", "h": "609fa23a6e", "text": "Quick, light mention only — no recap yet: after the break, the two walls in full detail, then (before revealing anything further) taking the Divine Plumbline Questionnaire so everyone has their own honest starting point Hold questions for the break — we'll pick up discussion after Close in prayer"}, {"ref": "D.2 §11 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.2-11-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "2d3d6b4775", "text": "☕ SHORT BREAK"}, {"ref": "D.2 §12 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.2-12-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "33900d3339", "text": "PART 2 — The Two Walls and the Four Situations"}, {"ref": "D.2 §12 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.2-12-1", "kind": "paragraph", "h": "1ac2cfeae4", "text": "Pacing note (≈1.5 hrs): opens with just a quick mention of what's ahead (and a chance to answer any plenum question from the break) — no recap needed yet. One exercise this session, positioned after the halfway point (Slide 17, the 8th of 12 slides in this Part) as a physical interruption to refocus. Closes with the full Day 2 recap and time for questions."}, {"ref": "D.2 §13 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.2-13-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "17c3432426", "text": "Slide 10 — Opening: What's Next"}, {"ref": "D.2 §13 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.2-13-1", "kind": "list", "h": "d4b7b35a2e", "text": "Open in prayer Quick, light mention only: \"we said the same wound can build two different walls — let's name them\" If a question came up in the break, take a moment to answer it in plenum here before moving on Set expectations: this session gets personal — questionnaire included"}, {"ref": "D.2 §14 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.2-14-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "5c1181af3e", "text": "Slide 11 — The Wall of Rejection"}, {"ref": "D.2 §14 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.2-14-1", "kind": "list", "h": "13a9c050ea", "text": "Cornerstone: unbelief Terminus (end state if unaddressed): despair, ultimately suicide — said gently and pastorally, not alarmist Introduce the three brick categories: Emotions, Intellect, Spirit"}, {"ref": "D.2 §15 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.2-15-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "6a5623b046", "text": "Slide 12 — Wall of Rejection — Brick Inventory"}, {"ref": "D.2 §15 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.2-15-1", "kind": "list", "h": "b5d02a3b68", "text": "Present the fuller brick list by category (Emotions / Intellect / Spirit) from the book Don't over-explain each one yet — this is a naming slide, not yet a diagnosis slide Point out a few that people will recognize immediately (e.g., Depression, Apathy, Inferiority)"}, {"ref": "D.2 §16 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.2-16-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "739ee24a56", "text": "Slide 13 — The Wall of Rebellion"}, {"ref": "D.2 §16 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.2-16-1", "kind": "list", "h": "d1580c3b30", "text": "Cornerstone: pride Terminus: hostility, ultimately homicide — again, said carefully and pastorally Same three-category structure: Emotions, Intellect, Spirit"}, {"ref": "D.2 §17 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.2-17-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "e054414c4f", "text": "Slide 14 — Wall of Rebellion — Brick Inventory"}, {"ref": "D.2 §17 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.2-17-1", "kind": "list", "h": "43f2dcf5f2", "text": "Present the fuller brick list by category Note the recognizable ones: Competitive, Controlling, Resentment, Criticism"}, {"ref": "D.2 §18 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.2-18-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "ab938d248c", "text": "Slide 15 — [story about xxx — a moment you clearly recognized one of these bricks operating in your own life, either wall]"}, {"ref": "D.2 §19 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.2-19-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "717ded0fbd", "text": "Slide 16 — Taking the Questionnaire"}, {"ref": "D.2 §19 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.2-19-1", "kind": "list", "h": "3b42b734f9", "text": "Hand out the Divine Plumbline Questionnaire (24 items, True/False) Instructions: answer honestly and quickly — first instinct, not overthought Explicitly note: scoring comes next, before any teaching on what the four types mean — so no one's score is \"coached\""}, {"ref": "D.2 §20 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.2-20-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "326e76e666", "text": "Slide 17 — [exercise] \"Both Walls\" Diagram Walkthrough & Scoring — MIDPOINT BREAK & REFOCUS"}, {"ref": "D.2 §20 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.2-20-1", "kind": "list", "h": "dc3e4aaaac", "text": "Show the \"Both Walls\" pyramid/brick diagram from the book In small groups, have people identify (privately) which side of the diagram feels more familiar — Rejection or Rebellion — without yet naming a specific brick Debrief lightly as a large group: it's common to feel \"both,\" and that's normal — no one is purely one wall Fold in scoring the questionnaire here too: count T's per letter (O, C, U, W), compare to each type's max, and give people a moment to total their own scores privately — a mirror, not a verdict Good movement/discussion break at the halfway point of this session"}, {"ref": "D.2 §21 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.2-21-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "0a46efb701", "text": "Slide 18 — Reveal: Four Typical Situations and Their Behaviors"}, {"ref": "D.2 §21 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.2-21-1", "kind": "list", "h": "81db9059df", "text": "Not personality: these are four typical wall situations, and the behaviors each one produces Now unpack what O / C / U / W actually stand for: O — Compliant (Saul) U — Can't Do It (Moses) W — Competitive (Jacob) C — Critical (Judas) Each tied to a biblical figure who displayed that behavior pattern"}, {"ref": "D.2 §22 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.2-22-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "1733d4f732", "text": "Slide 19 — The Four Situations in Depth"}, {"ref": "D.2 §22 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.2-22-1", "kind": "list", "h": "a2e7e5508c", "text": "For each type: core message (\"I'm not enough,\" \"I have to perform,\" etc.), key strengths, key weaknesses, manipulator-type (passive, indifferent, competitive, active) Emphasize: these are patterns to recognize, not boxes to live in"}, {"ref": "D.2 §23 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.2-23-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "b75ef6deda", "text": "Slide 20 — Not Mutually Exclusive — and Not Who You Are"}, {"ref": "D.2 §23 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.2-23-1", "kind": "list", "h": "76d779bfc2", "text": "Thompson's own conclusion: most people carry elements of more than one type, and can shift over time (he describes his own shift from Compliant toward Competitive) Reassure the room: having a \"high score\" in more than one type is completely normal State it plainly: a high score names a wall you built to protect yourself , not your identity — it's easy, over time, to mistake the brick pattern for the self underneath it, but it isn't the self Quick show of hands (optional, low-pressure): who scored highest in more than one type? Normalize it visibly"}, {"ref": "D.2 §24 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.2-24-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "9d3bdf7919", "text": "Slide 21 — Recap: Day 2 in Review + Questions"}, {"ref": "D.2 §24 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.2-24-1", "kind": "list", "h": "a80177962a", "text": "Full recap: Love Deficit → the generational pattern (visiting vs. blessing) → the two walls' brick inventories → your own questionnaire score → the four typical situations, their behaviors and their biblical archetypes Open the floor for questions from today's teaching Bridge to tomorrow: \"tomorrow we go behind the walls — what happens when these patterns go unaddressed\" Word of encouragement Close in prayer"}], "html": "<div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"6238fbf296\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.2 §0 ¶0\" data-sec=\"0\" id=\"p-D.2-0-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.2 §0 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.2 §0 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§0 ¶0</button><h1 data-body=\"1\">Day 2 — Love Deficit &amp; the Generational Pattern / The Two Walls and the Four Situations</h1></div><div class=\"blk blk-paragraph\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"2f2d1aa120\" data-kind=\"paragraph\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.2 §0 ¶1\" data-sec=\"0\" id=\"p-D.2-0-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.2 §0 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.2 §0 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§0 ¶1</button><p data-body=\"1\"><strong>Day overview:</strong> Morning session (Part 1) opens with a recap of Day 1, then covers the Love Deficit equations and the generational pattern (visiting vs. blessing, with sour grapes as its picture). Afternoon session (Part 2) covers the full brick inventory for both walls, administers the Divine Plumbline Questionnaire, then reveals the four typical situations and their behaviors.</p></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"e0aa7cba5b\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.2 §1 ¶0\" data-sec=\"1\" id=\"p-D.2-1-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.2 §1 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.2 §1 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§1 ¶0</button><h2 data-body=\"1\">PART 1 — Love Deficit and the Generational Pattern</h2></div><div class=\"blk blk-paragraph\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"dcdb25c0b6\" data-kind=\"paragraph\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.2 §1 ¶1\" data-sec=\"1\" id=\"p-D.2-1-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.2 §1 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.2 §1 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§1 ¶1</button><p data-body=\"1\"><strong>Pacing note (≈1.5 hrs):</strong> opens with the Day 1 recap and takes any questions that came up during the day. One exercise this session, positioned at the halfway point (Slide 5 of 9) as a physical interruption to refocus — not earlier. Closes with just a quick mention of what Part 2 covers — no full recap yet, questions held for the break.</p></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"2aeec36b3e\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.2 §2 ¶0\" data-sec=\"2\" id=\"p-D.2-2-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.2 §2 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.2 §2 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§2 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 1 — Yesterday, Quickly — and Any Questions</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"02a72c0303\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.2 §2 ¶1\" data-sec=\"2\" id=\"p-D.2-2-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.2 §2 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.2 §2 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§2 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Open in prayer</li>\n<li>One-slide reminder of Day 1: the plumbline as God's unmoving standard; behavior built like a wall; the seven false prophets; storms reveal what's already built</li>\n<li>Open the floor briefly for any questions that came up for people since yesterday</li>\n<li>Today's turn: \"now we look at exactly how the bricks get laid\"</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"4ff5506d9d\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.2 §3 ¶0\" data-sec=\"3\" id=\"p-D.2-3-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.2 §3 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.2 §3 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§3 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 2 — Identity Crisis: Where It Starts</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"bafddf496b\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.2 §3 ¶1\" data-sec=\"3\" id=\"p-D.2-3-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.2 §3 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.2 §3 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§3 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Every human being is born with a need to be loved, valued, and secure</li>\n<li>When that need isn't met — or isn't met consistently — an identity crisis begins</li>\n<li>This isn't about blaming parents; it's about understanding the mechanism</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"5a3f7e682e\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.2 §4 ¶0\" data-sec=\"4\" id=\"p-D.2-4-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.2 §4 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.2 §4 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§4 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 3 — The Love Deficit Equation</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"a8de07393b\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.2 §4 ¶1\" data-sec=\"4\" id=\"p-D.2-4-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.2 §4 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.2 §4 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§4 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Present the equation(s) from the book: unmet love/value/security needs → deficit → compensating behavior</li>\n<li>Explain simply: every person, at some level, is either resting in being loved or working to earn/prove it</li>\n<li><span class=\"ph-story\">[story about xxx — a time you caught yourself \"performing\" for love or approval rather than resting in being loved]</span></li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"9bdf5c833f\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.2 §5 ¶0\" data-sec=\"5\" id=\"p-D.2-5-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.2 §5 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.2 §5 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§5 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 4 — The Principle: Visiting vs. Blessing (Exodus 20:5-6)</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"aaf5a16964\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.2 §5 ¶1\" data-sec=\"5\" id=\"p-D.2-5-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.2 §5 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.2 §5 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§5 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>The law states the principle: God visits the iniquity of the fathers on the children, to the third and fourth generation (Exodus 20:5, 34:7; Deuteronomy 5:9) — this is where the concept of generational sin is rooted</li>\n<li>Unresolved pain and wrong patterns don't stay contained — they pass down</li>\n<li>But read the contrast in the same breath: steadfast love to thousands, to a thousand generations of those who love Him (Exodus 20:6; Deuteronomy 7:9). The blessing outreaches the visiting by far — that is what God is really saying</li>\n<li>The picture of how it passes down: sour grapes — \"The parents have eaten sour grapes, and the children's teeth are set on edge\" (Ezekiel 18:2, Jeremiah 31:29). People consuming and accepting what is wrong, and the next generation tasting it</li>\n<li>Not fatalism — naming the pattern is the first step to breaking it, and the greater weight is on the blessing side</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"38278b886d\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.2 §6 ¶0\" data-sec=\"6\" id=\"p-D.2-6-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.2 §6 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.2 §6 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§6 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 5 — <span class=\"ph-exercise\">[exercise]</span> Where Do You Feel the Deficit? — MIDPOINT BREAK &amp; REFOCUS</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"bccfd5a1cf\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.2 §6 ¶1\" data-sec=\"6\" id=\"p-D.2-6-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.2 §6 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.2 §6 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§6 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Quick physical/movement version: have people stand and move to a corner of the room representing where they feel most \"unfinished\" — being loved, being valued, or being secure</li>\n<li>Good stretch point at the halfway mark — no need to explain individual choices, just notice the room is genuinely split</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"c8627d014e\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.2 §7 ¶0\" data-sec=\"7\" id=\"p-D.2-7-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.2 §7 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.2 §7 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§7 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 6 — The Generational Symptom Chain</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"2ecb9988a1\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.2 §7 ¶1\" data-sec=\"7\" id=\"p-D.2-7-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.2 §7 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.2 §7 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§7 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Explain: the book cites U.S. military research on children whose fathers were away on deployment — the unresolved reactions of a child calcify over time into recognizable adult symptoms. The childhood reaction is the seed, the adult symptom is the fruit</li>\n<li>Show the table (childhood reaction → adult symptom):\n<ul>\n<li>Rage → Crime</li>\n<li>Denial &amp; Fantasy → Personality disorders</li>\n<li>Reunion attempts → Possessiveness</li>\n<li>Guilt → Depression (turned inward) / Delinquency (turned outward)</li>\n<li>Fear → Neuroses</li>\n<li>Impulse changes → Psychosomatic disorders</li>\n<li>Regression → Psychoses</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Example, worked through one line: a boy whose father is repeatedly absent feels guilt (\"it must be my fault\"). Turned inward, that guilt becomes depression; turned outward, it becomes acting out and delinquency. Same childhood reaction, two directions — which sets up the two walls</li>\n<li>Same pattern at everyday scale: a child who never gets the attention they need keeps trying to \"reunite\" — and as an adult clings to partners and friends (possessiveness)</li>\n<li>Key point: this is the mechanism of the generational pattern — unresolved reactions don't stay put, they harden and get passed on. At any link in the chain, truth can interrupt the cycle</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-paragraph\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"966c97fa5d\" data-kind=\"paragraph\" data-par=\"2\" data-ref=\"D.2 §7 ¶2\" data-sec=\"7\" id=\"p-D.2-7-2\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.2 §7 ¶2\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.2 §7 ¶2 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§7 ¶2</button><p data-body=\"1\"><em>Delivery note: the clinical terms (psychoses, personality disorders) are the book's research labels — present them as illustrations of how far unresolved reactions can travel, not as labels for anyone in the room.</em></p></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"f5df79a1b6\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.2 §8 ¶0\" data-sec=\"8\" id=\"p-D.2-8-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.2 §8 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.2 §8 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§8 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 7 — <span class=\"ph-story\">[story about xxx — a generational pattern you noticed in your own family line, and a moment where it started to break]</span></h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"367353b302\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.2 §9 ¶0\" data-sec=\"9\" id=\"p-D.2-9-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.2 §9 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.2 §9 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§9 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 8 — Two Roads From the Same Deficit</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"cc1e93a953\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.2 §9 ¶1\" data-sec=\"9\" id=\"p-D.2-9-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.2 §9 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.2 §9 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§9 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Preview: the same unmet need can produce two very different walls</li>\n<li>Rejection: withdrawing, believing \"I am not enough\"</li>\n<li>Rebellion: asserting, believing \"I will make myself enough\"</li>\n<li>Both are the same wound, answered in opposite directions — sets up the rest of the day</li>\n<li>We reinforce what those around us say: we want to belong, and we suffer from confirmation bias. First we think about what people say, then we accept it, agree with it, believe it — and finally speak it out over ourselves</li>\n<li>But this can be broken: Jesus is the atonement for our sins, and that is the root that lets you and me break our agreement with what was said — and not pay the price for it</li>\n<li>\"Anything you say can be held against you in a court of law\" — not if I recant it. Jesus has already paid for it, so the accusation cannot stand</li>\n<li>It gets replaced with what God says about us — every day, every time</li>\n<li>Sets up Days 4–5: a place to bring our daily struggles, and the words to break anything anyone has spoken over us</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-paragraph\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"12f6d4dc83\" data-kind=\"paragraph\" data-par=\"2\" data-ref=\"D.2 §9 ¶2\" data-sec=\"9\" id=\"p-D.2-9-2\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.2 §9 ¶2\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.2 §9 ¶2 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§9 ¶2</button><p data-body=\"1\"><em>Delivery note: the courtroom line works best with a light touch and a smile (\"Tell Jesus the charge still stands!\") — humor makes the truth land, not lighter.</em></p></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"d561b753cc\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.2 §10 ¶0\" data-sec=\"10\" id=\"p-D.2-10-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.2 §10 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.2 §10 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§10 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 9 — What's Coming in Part 2</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"609fa23a6e\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.2 §10 ¶1\" data-sec=\"10\" id=\"p-D.2-10-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.2 §10 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.2 §10 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§10 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Quick, light mention only — no recap yet: after the break, the two walls in full detail, then (before revealing anything further) taking the Divine Plumbline Questionnaire so everyone has their own honest starting point</li>\n<li>Hold questions for the break — we'll pick up discussion after</li>\n<li>Close in prayer</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"2d3d6b4775\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.2 §11 ¶0\" data-sec=\"11\" id=\"p-D.2-11-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.2 §11 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.2 §11 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§11 ¶0</button><h2 data-body=\"1\">☕ SHORT BREAK</h2></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"33900d3339\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.2 §12 ¶0\" data-sec=\"12\" id=\"p-D.2-12-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.2 §12 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.2 §12 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§12 ¶0</button><h2 data-body=\"1\">PART 2 — The Two Walls and the Four Situations</h2></div><div class=\"blk blk-paragraph\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"1ac2cfeae4\" data-kind=\"paragraph\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.2 §12 ¶1\" data-sec=\"12\" id=\"p-D.2-12-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.2 §12 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.2 §12 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§12 ¶1</button><p data-body=\"1\"><strong>Pacing note (≈1.5 hrs):</strong> opens with just a quick mention of what's ahead (and a chance to answer any plenum question from the break) — no recap needed yet. One exercise this session, positioned after the halfway point (Slide 17, the 8th of 12 slides in this Part) as a physical interruption to refocus. Closes with the full Day 2 recap and time for questions.</p></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"17c3432426\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.2 §13 ¶0\" data-sec=\"13\" id=\"p-D.2-13-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.2 §13 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.2 §13 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§13 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 10 — Opening: What's Next</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"d4b7b35a2e\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.2 §13 ¶1\" data-sec=\"13\" id=\"p-D.2-13-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.2 §13 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.2 §13 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§13 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Open in prayer</li>\n<li>Quick, light mention only: \"we said the same wound can build two different walls — let's name them\"</li>\n<li>If a question came up in the break, take a moment to answer it in plenum here before moving on</li>\n<li>Set expectations: this session gets personal — questionnaire included</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"5c1181af3e\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.2 §14 ¶0\" data-sec=\"14\" id=\"p-D.2-14-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.2 §14 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.2 §14 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§14 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 11 — The Wall of Rejection</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"13a9c050ea\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.2 §14 ¶1\" data-sec=\"14\" id=\"p-D.2-14-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.2 §14 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.2 §14 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§14 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Cornerstone: unbelief</li>\n<li>Terminus (end state if unaddressed): despair, ultimately suicide — said gently and pastorally, not alarmist</li>\n<li>Introduce the three brick categories: Emotions, Intellect, Spirit</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"6a5623b046\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.2 §15 ¶0\" data-sec=\"15\" id=\"p-D.2-15-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.2 §15 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.2 §15 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§15 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 12 — Wall of Rejection — Brick Inventory</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"b5d02a3b68\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.2 §15 ¶1\" data-sec=\"15\" id=\"p-D.2-15-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.2 §15 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.2 §15 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§15 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Present the fuller brick list by category (Emotions / Intellect / Spirit) from the book</li>\n<li>Don't over-explain each one yet — this is a naming slide, not yet a diagnosis slide</li>\n<li>Point out a few that people will recognize immediately (e.g., Depression, Apathy, Inferiority)</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"739ee24a56\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.2 §16 ¶0\" data-sec=\"16\" id=\"p-D.2-16-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.2 §16 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.2 §16 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§16 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 13 — The Wall of Rebellion</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"d1580c3b30\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.2 §16 ¶1\" data-sec=\"16\" id=\"p-D.2-16-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.2 §16 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.2 §16 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§16 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Cornerstone: pride</li>\n<li>Terminus: hostility, ultimately homicide — again, said carefully and pastorally</li>\n<li>Same three-category structure: Emotions, Intellect, Spirit</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"e054414c4f\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.2 §17 ¶0\" data-sec=\"17\" id=\"p-D.2-17-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.2 §17 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.2 §17 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§17 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 14 — Wall of Rebellion — Brick Inventory</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"43f2dcf5f2\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.2 §17 ¶1\" data-sec=\"17\" id=\"p-D.2-17-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.2 §17 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.2 §17 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§17 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Present the fuller brick list by category</li>\n<li>Note the recognizable ones: Competitive, Controlling, Resentment, Criticism</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"ab938d248c\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.2 §18 ¶0\" data-sec=\"18\" id=\"p-D.2-18-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.2 §18 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.2 §18 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§18 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 15 — <span class=\"ph-story\">[story about xxx — a moment you clearly recognized one of these bricks operating in your own life, either wall]</span></h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"717ded0fbd\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.2 §19 ¶0\" data-sec=\"19\" id=\"p-D.2-19-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.2 §19 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.2 §19 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§19 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 16 — Taking the Questionnaire</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"3b42b734f9\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.2 §19 ¶1\" data-sec=\"19\" id=\"p-D.2-19-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.2 §19 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.2 §19 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§19 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Hand out the Divine Plumbline Questionnaire (24 items, True/False)</li>\n<li>Instructions: answer honestly and quickly — first instinct, not overthought</li>\n<li>Explicitly note: scoring comes next, before any teaching on what the four types mean — so no one's score is \"coached\"</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"326e76e666\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.2 §20 ¶0\" data-sec=\"20\" id=\"p-D.2-20-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.2 §20 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.2 §20 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§20 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 17 — <span class=\"ph-exercise\">[exercise]</span> \"Both Walls\" Diagram Walkthrough &amp; Scoring — MIDPOINT BREAK &amp; REFOCUS</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"dc3e4aaaac\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.2 §20 ¶1\" data-sec=\"20\" id=\"p-D.2-20-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.2 §20 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.2 §20 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§20 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Show the \"Both Walls\" pyramid/brick diagram from the book</li>\n<li>In small groups, have people identify (privately) which side of the diagram feels more familiar — Rejection or Rebellion — without yet naming a specific brick</li>\n<li>Debrief lightly as a large group: it's common to feel \"both,\" and that's normal — no one is purely one wall</li>\n<li>Fold in scoring the questionnaire here too: count T's per letter (O, C, U, W), compare to each type's max, and give people a moment to total their own scores privately — a mirror, not a verdict</li>\n<li>Good movement/discussion break at the halfway point of this session</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"0a46efb701\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.2 §21 ¶0\" data-sec=\"21\" id=\"p-D.2-21-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.2 §21 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.2 §21 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§21 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 18 — Reveal: Four Typical Situations and Their Behaviors</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"81db9059df\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.2 §21 ¶1\" data-sec=\"21\" id=\"p-D.2-21-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.2 §21 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.2 §21 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§21 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Not personality: these are four typical wall situations, and the behaviors each one produces</li>\n<li>Now unpack what O / C / U / W actually stand for:\n<ul>\n<li><strong>O — Compliant</strong> (Saul)</li>\n<li><strong>U — Can't Do It</strong> (Moses)</li>\n<li><strong>W — Competitive</strong> (Jacob)</li>\n<li><strong>C — Critical</strong> (Judas)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Each tied to a biblical figure who displayed that behavior pattern</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"1733d4f732\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.2 §22 ¶0\" data-sec=\"22\" id=\"p-D.2-22-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.2 §22 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.2 §22 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§22 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 19 — The Four Situations in Depth</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"a2e7e5508c\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.2 §22 ¶1\" data-sec=\"22\" id=\"p-D.2-22-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.2 §22 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.2 §22 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§22 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>For each type: core message (\"I'm not enough,\" \"I have to perform,\" etc.), key strengths, key weaknesses, manipulator-type (passive, indifferent, competitive, active)</li>\n<li>Emphasize: these are patterns to recognize, not boxes to live in</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"b75ef6deda\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.2 §23 ¶0\" data-sec=\"23\" id=\"p-D.2-23-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.2 §23 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.2 §23 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§23 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 20 — Not Mutually Exclusive — and Not Who You Are</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"76d779bfc2\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.2 §23 ¶1\" data-sec=\"23\" id=\"p-D.2-23-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.2 §23 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.2 §23 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§23 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Thompson's own conclusion: most people carry elements of more than one type, and can shift over time (he describes his own shift from Compliant toward Competitive)</li>\n<li>Reassure the room: having a \"high score\" in more than one type is completely normal</li>\n<li>State it plainly: a high score names a <strong>wall you built to protect yourself</strong>, not your identity — it's easy, over time, to mistake the brick pattern for the self underneath it, but it isn't the self</li>\n<li>Quick show of hands (optional, low-pressure): who scored highest in more than one type? Normalize it visibly</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"9d3bdf7919\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.2 §24 ¶0\" data-sec=\"24\" id=\"p-D.2-24-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.2 §24 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.2 §24 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§24 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 21 — Recap: Day 2 in Review + Questions</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"a80177962a\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.2 §24 ¶1\" data-sec=\"24\" id=\"p-D.2-24-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.2 §24 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.2 §24 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§24 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Full recap: Love Deficit → the generational pattern (visiting vs. blessing) → the two walls' brick inventories → your own questionnaire score → the four typical situations, their behaviors and their biblical archetypes</li>\n<li>Open the floor for questions from today's teaching</li>\n<li>Bridge to tomorrow: \"tomorrow we go behind the walls — what happens when these patterns go unaddressed\"</li>\n<li>Word of encouragement</li>\n<li>Close in prayer</li>\n</ul></div>"}, {"id": "D.3", "title": "Day 3 — The Lust Trap (Flight Swing) / The Pride Trap (Fight Swing) and the Final Swing", "order": 4, "metadata": {"source": "src/tdp-day3.md", "kind": "Day 3"}, "file": "d3-day3.md", "notes": ["<p>original (Q3, 2026-11-16): \"- One-slide reminder of Day 2: Love Deficit and the generational pattern → the two walls' bricks → the questionnaire → the four structures and their behaviours (Compliant, Can't Do It, Competitive, Critical)\"</p>\n"], "blocks": [{"ref": "D.3 §0 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.3-0-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "b2eea0ac02", "text": "Day 3 — The Lust Trap (Flight Swing) / The Pride Trap (Fight Swing) and the Final Swing"}, {"ref": "D.3 §0 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.3-0-1", "kind": "paragraph", "h": "4db790b5f9", "text": "Day overview: Morning session (Part 1) opens with a recap of Day 2, then explores what happens \"behind\" the Wall of Rejection when it goes unaddressed — the Lust Trap and the Flight Swing. Afternoon session (Part 2) covers the mirror-image pattern behind the Wall of Rebellion, plus the convergence point where both swings can lead toward the same destructive end."}, {"ref": "D.3 §1 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.3-1-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "a2be8523f0", "text": "PART 1 — The Lust Trap (Flight Swing)"}, {"ref": "D.3 §1 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.3-1-1", "kind": "paragraph", "h": "ab19fc935e", "text": "Pacing note (≈1.5 hrs): opens with the Day 2 recap and takes any questions that came up during the day. One exercise this session, positioned at the halfway point (Slide 5 of 9) as a physical interruption to refocus — not earlier. Closes with just a quick mention of what Part 2 covers — no full recap yet, questions held for the break."}, {"ref": "D.3 §2 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.3-2-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "2aeec36b3e", "text": "Slide 1 — Yesterday, Quickly — and Any Questions"}, {"ref": "D.3 §2 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.3-2-1", "kind": "list", "h": "2d8a2357d1", "text": "Open in prayer One-slide reminder of Day 2: Love Deficit and the generational pattern → the two walls' bricks → the questionnaire → the four situations and their behaviors (Compliant, Can't Do It, Competitive, Critical) Open the floor briefly for any questions that came up for people since yesterday Today's turn: \"what happens when these walls stay unaddressed long enough\""}, {"ref": "D.3 §3 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.3-3-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "1458056eab", "text": "Slide 2 — Behind the Wall"}, {"ref": "D.3 §3 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.3-3-1", "kind": "list", "h": "2f14d34b0c", "text": "Introduce the idea that walls aren't static — unaddressed pain pushes people toward coping mechanisms Two main patterns: the Flight response (behind Rejection) and the Fight response (behind Rebellion) Today: Flight. This afternoon we pick up with Fight"}, {"ref": "D.3 §4 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.3-4-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "f47d06f413", "text": "Slide 3 — The Lust Trap"}, {"ref": "D.3 §4 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.3-4-1", "kind": "list", "h": "1150508f06", "text": "Define \"lust\" broadly here — not only sexual, but any craving used to numb or escape pain The trap: what promises relief actually deepens the wound it's trying to escape Connects directly back to the Wall of Rejection's unbelief cornerstone — \"I am not enough, so I'll fill the gap myself\""}, {"ref": "D.3 §5 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.3-5-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "239a973d5a", "text": "Slide 4 — [story about xxx — a personal or observed example of reaching for something (not necessarily addiction — could be food, media, approval-seeking, etc.) to numb pain rather than face it]"}, {"ref": "D.3 §6 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.3-6-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "df9a72ff51", "text": "Slide 5 — [exercise] Naming the Swing — MIDPOINT BREAK & REFOCUS"}, {"ref": "D.3 §6 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.3-6-1", "kind": "list", "h": "dbb9acab32", "text": "Quiet, personal reflection paired with a brief stand-and-stretch: think of an area where you notice yourself swinging between two extremes (not necessarily food) — indulge vs. deny, engage vs. avoid Not shared aloud — purely for personal insight, but gives a physical break at the halfway mark"}, {"ref": "D.3 §7 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.3-7-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "33be068250", "text": "Slide 6 — The Flight Swing"}, {"ref": "D.3 §7 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.3-7-1", "kind": "list", "h": "f3f715b2d2", "text": "Explain the \"swing\" metaphor: an oscillation between two extremes rather than a stable middle Flight swing: between numbing/indulgence and self-punishment/withdrawal Neither extreme resolves the underlying wound — both are reactions to it"}, {"ref": "D.3 §8 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.3-8-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "a222da6013", "text": "Slide 7 — Eating Disorders as a Case Study"}, {"ref": "D.3 §8 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.3-8-1", "kind": "list", "h": "0207b92ee6", "text": "Use the book's discussion of eating disorders as a concrete illustration of the Flight Swing in action Control/restriction on one side, bingeing/numbing on the other — same underlying wound, different expressions"}, {"ref": "D.3 §8 ¶2", "anchor": "p-D.3-8-2", "kind": "paragraph", "h": "1db302afbe", "text": "Delivery note: likely someone in the room has a personal connection to this topic — a pastoral, unhurried tone here matters more than the content itself."}, {"ref": "D.3 §9 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.3-9-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "93c4bec12c", "text": "Slide 8 — Solomon's Opponent-Process Theory"}, {"ref": "D.3 §9 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.3-9-1", "kind": "list", "h": "dee4932e2c", "text": "Introduce Solomon's psychological theory referenced in the book: every strong pleasure is followed by an opposite \"counter-emotion,\" and repeated exposure trains the swing to get more extreme Simple example: the crash after a high, and why the craving intensifies over time Bridges the psychological research to the spiritual pattern already described Close with a quick verbal prompt (not a break activity, stays seated): in general terms, what has helped people you know break an unhealthy pattern?"}, {"ref": "D.3 §10 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.3-10-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "d561b753cc", "text": "Slide 9 — What's Coming in Part 2"}, {"ref": "D.3 §10 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.3-10-1", "kind": "list", "h": "3c3a671e18", "text": "Quick, light mention only — no recap yet: after the break, the mirror-image pattern behind the Wall of Rebellion — the Pride Trap and Fight Swing Hold questions for the break — we'll pick up discussion after Close in prayer"}, {"ref": "D.3 §11 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.3-11-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "2d3d6b4775", "text": "☕ SHORT BREAK"}, {"ref": "D.3 §12 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.3-12-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "19f3a9f172", "text": "PART 2 — The Pride Trap (Fight Swing) and the Final Swing"}, {"ref": "D.3 §12 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.3-12-1", "kind": "paragraph", "h": "4fbab66f9d", "text": "Pacing note (≈1.5 hrs): opens with just a quick mention of what's ahead (and a chance to answer any plenum question from the break) — no recap needed yet. One exercise this session, positioned after the halfway point (Slide 15, the 5th of 9 slides in this Part) as a physical interruption to refocus. Closes with the full Day 3 recap and time for questions."}, {"ref": "D.3 §13 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.3-13-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "17c3432426", "text": "Slide 10 — Opening: What's Next"}, {"ref": "D.3 §13 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.3-13-1", "kind": "list", "h": "ed0396605a", "text": "Open in prayer Quick, light mention only: \"we looked at Flight this morning — now let's look at its mirror image, Fight\" If a question came up in the break, take a moment to answer it in plenum here before moving on Reminder: same underlying wound, opposite direction of response"}, {"ref": "D.3 §14 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.3-14-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "df4eba4cdf", "text": "Slide 11 — The Pride Trap"}, {"ref": "D.3 §14 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.3-14-1", "kind": "list", "h": "72d844f68a", "text": "Define pride here as self-sufficiency — \"I don't need to be loved, I'll make myself matter\" Connects back to the Wall of Rebellion's pride cornerstone The trap: the more control/achievement is grasped for, the less secure the person actually feels underneath"}, {"ref": "D.3 §15 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.3-15-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "58d9cb0328", "text": "Slide 12 — [story about xxx — a time you (or someone close to you) tried to out-perform or out-control a feeling of inadequacy, and what it actually cost]"}, {"ref": "D.3 §16 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.3-16-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "c72b0b838a", "text": "Slide 13 — The Fight Swing"}, {"ref": "D.3 §16 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.3-16-1", "kind": "list", "h": "378448ae18", "text": "Same oscillation structure as this morning's Flight Swing, but expressed as control/domination on one side and collapse/burnout on the other Illustrate with a simple, recognizable example (workaholism swinging into burnout, or control swinging into an outburst)"}, {"ref": "D.3 §17 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.3-17-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "2c6c40111c", "text": "Slide 14 — Secular Humanism's \"Superman Philosophy\""}, {"ref": "D.3 §17 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.3-17-1", "kind": "list", "h": "c8d7a402c9", "text": "Present the book's critique of the cultural \"you can be anything, master your own destiny\" narrative Point out how this philosophy can quietly reinforce the Rebellion wall by treating self-sufficiency as the goal rather than the problem Contrast with the biblical picture of strength found in humility and dependence on God"}, {"ref": "D.3 §18 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.3-18-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "729237f5a8", "text": "Slide 15 — [exercise] Spot the Superman Message — MIDPOINT BREAK & REFOCUS"}, {"ref": "D.3 §18 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.3-18-1", "kind": "list", "h": "f5d93a3fb8", "text": "Quick group activity: look at a few real advertising slogans or popular quotes (\"Be your own hero,\" \"You don't need anyone\") and discuss, as a group, how each one either affirms healthy confidence or quietly feeds self-sufficiency Keep it light and observational, not preachy — a good energetic reset at the halfway point"}, {"ref": "D.3 §19 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.3-19-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "c979f1d037", "text": "Slide 16 — The Final Swing: Where Both Walls Can Lead"}, {"ref": "D.3 §19 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.3-19-1", "kind": "list", "h": "225bae2934", "text": "Explain the book's \"soul swings\" concept: both the Flight and Fight patterns, left unaddressed long enough, can converge toward the same destructive endpoint — described in the book as a kind of double-mindedness Purpose of naming this: not to alarm, but to show why the restoration process (starting tomorrow) matters Reiterate: naming the pattern is not a diagnosis of anyone in the room — it's a warning label on an unaddressed road, not a prediction"}, {"ref": "D.3 §19 ¶2", "anchor": "p-D.3-19-2", "kind": "paragraph", "h": "33da7cd010", "text": "Delivery note: a careful, pastoral tone matters more here than on most other slides this week."}, {"ref": "D.3 §20 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.3-20-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "918bfe6b80", "text": "Slide 17 — Why Awareness Isn't Enough"}, {"ref": "D.3 §20 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.3-20-1", "kind": "list", "h": "c3595f7022", "text": "Bridge point: understanding the walls and the swings intellectually doesn't dismantle them What's needed is restoration, not just recognition — sets up the whole second half of the week Quiet moment: without sharing, let each person silently name (in their own mind or journal) one pattern from today they'd like to see change"}, {"ref": "D.3 §21 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.3-21-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "7c87da0773", "text": "Slide 18 — Recap: Day 3 in Review + Questions"}, {"ref": "D.3 §21 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.3-21-1", "kind": "list", "h": "ba1f1f2dc2", "text": "Full recap: behind the Wall of Rejection — the Lust Trap and Flight Swing; behind the Wall of Rebellion — the Pride Trap and Fight Swing; both capable of converging toward the same destructive end if left unaddressed Open the floor for questions from today's teaching Bridge to tomorrow: \"tomorrow, we stop diagnosing and start rebuilding\" Word of encouragement Close in prayer"}], "html": "<div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"b2eea0ac02\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.3 §0 ¶0\" data-sec=\"0\" id=\"p-D.3-0-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.3 §0 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.3 §0 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§0 ¶0</button><h1 data-body=\"1\">Day 3 — The Lust Trap (Flight Swing) / The Pride Trap (Fight Swing) and the Final Swing</h1></div><div class=\"blk blk-paragraph\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"4db790b5f9\" data-kind=\"paragraph\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.3 §0 ¶1\" data-sec=\"0\" id=\"p-D.3-0-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.3 §0 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.3 §0 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§0 ¶1</button><p data-body=\"1\"><strong>Day overview:</strong> Morning session (Part 1) opens with a recap of Day 2, then explores what happens \"behind\" the Wall of Rejection when it goes unaddressed — the Lust Trap and the Flight Swing. Afternoon session (Part 2) covers the mirror-image pattern behind the Wall of Rebellion, plus the convergence point where both swings can lead toward the same destructive end.</p></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"a2be8523f0\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.3 §1 ¶0\" data-sec=\"1\" id=\"p-D.3-1-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.3 §1 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.3 §1 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§1 ¶0</button><h2 data-body=\"1\">PART 1 — The Lust Trap (Flight Swing)</h2></div><div class=\"blk blk-paragraph\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"ab19fc935e\" data-kind=\"paragraph\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.3 §1 ¶1\" data-sec=\"1\" id=\"p-D.3-1-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.3 §1 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.3 §1 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§1 ¶1</button><p data-body=\"1\"><strong>Pacing note (≈1.5 hrs):</strong> opens with the Day 2 recap and takes any questions that came up during the day. One exercise this session, positioned at the halfway point (Slide 5 of 9) as a physical interruption to refocus — not earlier. Closes with just a quick mention of what Part 2 covers — no full recap yet, questions held for the break.</p></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"2aeec36b3e\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.3 §2 ¶0\" data-sec=\"2\" id=\"p-D.3-2-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.3 §2 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.3 §2 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§2 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 1 — Yesterday, Quickly — and Any Questions</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"2d8a2357d1\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.3 §2 ¶1\" data-sec=\"2\" id=\"p-D.3-2-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.3 §2 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.3 §2 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§2 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Open in prayer</li>\n<li>One-slide reminder of Day 2: Love Deficit and the generational pattern → the two walls' bricks → the questionnaire → the four situations and their behaviors (Compliant, Can't Do It, Competitive, Critical)</li>\n<li>Open the floor briefly for any questions that came up for people since yesterday</li>\n<li>Today's turn: \"what happens when these walls stay unaddressed long enough\"</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"1458056eab\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.3 §3 ¶0\" data-sec=\"3\" id=\"p-D.3-3-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.3 §3 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.3 §3 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§3 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 2 — Behind the Wall</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"2f14d34b0c\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.3 §3 ¶1\" data-sec=\"3\" id=\"p-D.3-3-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.3 §3 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.3 §3 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§3 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Introduce the idea that walls aren't static — unaddressed pain pushes people toward coping mechanisms</li>\n<li>Two main patterns: the Flight response (behind Rejection) and the Fight response (behind Rebellion)</li>\n<li>Today: Flight. This afternoon we pick up with Fight</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"f47d06f413\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.3 §4 ¶0\" data-sec=\"4\" id=\"p-D.3-4-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.3 §4 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.3 §4 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§4 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 3 — The Lust Trap</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"1150508f06\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.3 §4 ¶1\" data-sec=\"4\" id=\"p-D.3-4-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.3 §4 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.3 §4 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§4 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Define \"lust\" broadly here — not only sexual, but any craving used to numb or escape pain</li>\n<li>The trap: what promises relief actually deepens the wound it's trying to escape</li>\n<li>Connects directly back to the Wall of Rejection's unbelief cornerstone — \"I am not enough, so I'll fill the gap myself\"</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"239a973d5a\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.3 §5 ¶0\" data-sec=\"5\" id=\"p-D.3-5-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.3 §5 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.3 §5 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§5 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 4 — <span class=\"ph-story\">[story about xxx — a personal or observed example of reaching for something (not necessarily addiction — could be food, media, approval-seeking, etc.) to numb pain rather than face it]</span></h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"df9a72ff51\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.3 §6 ¶0\" data-sec=\"6\" id=\"p-D.3-6-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.3 §6 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.3 §6 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§6 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 5 — <span class=\"ph-exercise\">[exercise]</span> Naming the Swing — MIDPOINT BREAK &amp; REFOCUS</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"dbb9acab32\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.3 §6 ¶1\" data-sec=\"6\" id=\"p-D.3-6-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.3 §6 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.3 §6 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§6 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Quiet, personal reflection paired with a brief stand-and-stretch: think of an area where you notice yourself swinging between two extremes (not necessarily food) — indulge vs. deny, engage vs. avoid</li>\n<li>Not shared aloud — purely for personal insight, but gives a physical break at the halfway mark</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"33be068250\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.3 §7 ¶0\" data-sec=\"7\" id=\"p-D.3-7-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.3 §7 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.3 §7 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§7 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 6 — The Flight Swing</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"f3f715b2d2\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.3 §7 ¶1\" data-sec=\"7\" id=\"p-D.3-7-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.3 §7 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.3 §7 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§7 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Explain the \"swing\" metaphor: an oscillation between two extremes rather than a stable middle</li>\n<li>Flight swing: between numbing/indulgence and self-punishment/withdrawal</li>\n<li>Neither extreme resolves the underlying wound — both are reactions to it</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"a222da6013\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.3 §8 ¶0\" data-sec=\"8\" id=\"p-D.3-8-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.3 §8 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.3 §8 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§8 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 7 — Eating Disorders as a Case Study</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"0207b92ee6\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.3 §8 ¶1\" data-sec=\"8\" id=\"p-D.3-8-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.3 §8 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.3 §8 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§8 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Use the book's discussion of eating disorders as a concrete illustration of the Flight Swing in action</li>\n<li>Control/restriction on one side, bingeing/numbing on the other — same underlying wound, different expressions</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-paragraph\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"1db302afbe\" data-kind=\"paragraph\" data-par=\"2\" data-ref=\"D.3 §8 ¶2\" data-sec=\"8\" id=\"p-D.3-8-2\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.3 §8 ¶2\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.3 §8 ¶2 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§8 ¶2</button><p data-body=\"1\"><em>Delivery note: likely someone in the room has a personal connection to this topic — a pastoral, unhurried tone here matters more than the content itself.</em></p></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"93c4bec12c\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.3 §9 ¶0\" data-sec=\"9\" id=\"p-D.3-9-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.3 §9 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.3 §9 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§9 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 8 — Solomon's Opponent-Process Theory</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"dee4932e2c\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.3 §9 ¶1\" data-sec=\"9\" id=\"p-D.3-9-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.3 §9 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.3 §9 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§9 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Introduce Solomon's psychological theory referenced in the book: every strong pleasure is followed by an opposite \"counter-emotion,\" and repeated exposure trains the swing to get more extreme</li>\n<li>Simple example: the crash after a high, and why the craving intensifies over time</li>\n<li>Bridges the psychological research to the spiritual pattern already described</li>\n<li>Close with a quick verbal prompt (not a break activity, stays seated): in general terms, what has helped people you know break an unhealthy pattern?</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"d561b753cc\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.3 §10 ¶0\" data-sec=\"10\" id=\"p-D.3-10-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.3 §10 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.3 §10 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§10 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 9 — What's Coming in Part 2</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"3c3a671e18\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.3 §10 ¶1\" data-sec=\"10\" id=\"p-D.3-10-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.3 §10 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.3 §10 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§10 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Quick, light mention only — no recap yet: after the break, the mirror-image pattern behind the Wall of Rebellion — the Pride Trap and Fight Swing</li>\n<li>Hold questions for the break — we'll pick up discussion after</li>\n<li>Close in prayer</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"2d3d6b4775\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.3 §11 ¶0\" data-sec=\"11\" id=\"p-D.3-11-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.3 §11 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.3 §11 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§11 ¶0</button><h2 data-body=\"1\">☕ SHORT BREAK</h2></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"19f3a9f172\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.3 §12 ¶0\" data-sec=\"12\" id=\"p-D.3-12-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.3 §12 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.3 §12 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§12 ¶0</button><h2 data-body=\"1\">PART 2 — The Pride Trap (Fight Swing) and the Final Swing</h2></div><div class=\"blk blk-paragraph\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"4fbab66f9d\" data-kind=\"paragraph\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.3 §12 ¶1\" data-sec=\"12\" id=\"p-D.3-12-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.3 §12 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.3 §12 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§12 ¶1</button><p data-body=\"1\"><strong>Pacing note (≈1.5 hrs):</strong> opens with just a quick mention of what's ahead (and a chance to answer any plenum question from the break) — no recap needed yet. One exercise this session, positioned after the halfway point (Slide 15, the 5th of 9 slides in this Part) as a physical interruption to refocus. Closes with the full Day 3 recap and time for questions.</p></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"17c3432426\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.3 §13 ¶0\" data-sec=\"13\" id=\"p-D.3-13-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.3 §13 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.3 §13 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§13 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 10 — Opening: What's Next</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"ed0396605a\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.3 §13 ¶1\" data-sec=\"13\" id=\"p-D.3-13-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.3 §13 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.3 §13 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§13 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Open in prayer</li>\n<li>Quick, light mention only: \"we looked at Flight this morning — now let's look at its mirror image, Fight\"</li>\n<li>If a question came up in the break, take a moment to answer it in plenum here before moving on</li>\n<li>Reminder: same underlying wound, opposite direction of response</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"df4eba4cdf\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.3 §14 ¶0\" data-sec=\"14\" id=\"p-D.3-14-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.3 §14 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.3 §14 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§14 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 11 — The Pride Trap</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"72d844f68a\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.3 §14 ¶1\" data-sec=\"14\" id=\"p-D.3-14-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.3 §14 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.3 §14 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§14 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Define pride here as self-sufficiency — \"I don't need to be loved, I'll make myself matter\"</li>\n<li>Connects back to the Wall of Rebellion's pride cornerstone</li>\n<li>The trap: the more control/achievement is grasped for, the less secure the person actually feels underneath</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"58d9cb0328\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.3 §15 ¶0\" data-sec=\"15\" id=\"p-D.3-15-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.3 §15 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.3 §15 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§15 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 12 — <span class=\"ph-story\">[story about xxx — a time you (or someone close to you) tried to out-perform or out-control a feeling of inadequacy, and what it actually cost]</span></h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"c72b0b838a\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.3 §16 ¶0\" data-sec=\"16\" id=\"p-D.3-16-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.3 §16 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.3 §16 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§16 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 13 — The Fight Swing</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"378448ae18\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.3 §16 ¶1\" data-sec=\"16\" id=\"p-D.3-16-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.3 §16 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.3 §16 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§16 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Same oscillation structure as this morning's Flight Swing, but expressed as control/domination on one side and collapse/burnout on the other</li>\n<li>Illustrate with a simple, recognizable example (workaholism swinging into burnout, or control swinging into an outburst)</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"2c6c40111c\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.3 §17 ¶0\" data-sec=\"17\" id=\"p-D.3-17-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.3 §17 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.3 §17 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§17 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 14 — Secular Humanism's \"Superman Philosophy\"</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"c8d7a402c9\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.3 §17 ¶1\" data-sec=\"17\" id=\"p-D.3-17-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.3 §17 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.3 §17 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§17 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Present the book's critique of the cultural \"you can be anything, master your own destiny\" narrative</li>\n<li>Point out how this philosophy can quietly reinforce the Rebellion wall by treating self-sufficiency as the goal rather than the problem</li>\n<li>Contrast with the biblical picture of strength found in humility and dependence on God</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"729237f5a8\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.3 §18 ¶0\" data-sec=\"18\" id=\"p-D.3-18-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.3 §18 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.3 §18 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§18 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 15 — <span class=\"ph-exercise\">[exercise]</span> Spot the Superman Message — MIDPOINT BREAK &amp; REFOCUS</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"f5d93a3fb8\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.3 §18 ¶1\" data-sec=\"18\" id=\"p-D.3-18-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.3 §18 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.3 §18 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§18 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Quick group activity: look at a few real advertising slogans or popular quotes (\"Be your own hero,\" \"You don't need anyone\") and discuss, as a group, how each one either affirms healthy confidence or quietly feeds self-sufficiency</li>\n<li>Keep it light and observational, not preachy — a good energetic reset at the halfway point</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"c979f1d037\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.3 §19 ¶0\" data-sec=\"19\" id=\"p-D.3-19-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.3 §19 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.3 §19 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§19 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 16 — The Final Swing: Where Both Walls Can Lead</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"225bae2934\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.3 §19 ¶1\" data-sec=\"19\" id=\"p-D.3-19-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.3 §19 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.3 §19 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§19 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Explain the book's \"soul swings\" concept: both the Flight and Fight patterns, left unaddressed long enough, can converge toward the same destructive endpoint — described in the book as a kind of double-mindedness</li>\n<li>Purpose of naming this: not to alarm, but to show why the restoration process (starting tomorrow) matters</li>\n<li>Reiterate: naming the pattern is not a diagnosis of anyone in the room — it's a warning label on an unaddressed road, not a prediction</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-paragraph\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"33da7cd010\" data-kind=\"paragraph\" data-par=\"2\" data-ref=\"D.3 §19 ¶2\" data-sec=\"19\" id=\"p-D.3-19-2\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.3 §19 ¶2\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.3 §19 ¶2 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§19 ¶2</button><p data-body=\"1\"><em>Delivery note: a careful, pastoral tone matters more here than on most other slides this week.</em></p></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"918bfe6b80\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.3 §20 ¶0\" data-sec=\"20\" id=\"p-D.3-20-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.3 §20 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.3 §20 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§20 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 17 — Why Awareness Isn't Enough</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"c3595f7022\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.3 §20 ¶1\" data-sec=\"20\" id=\"p-D.3-20-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.3 §20 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.3 §20 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§20 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Bridge point: understanding the walls and the swings intellectually doesn't dismantle them</li>\n<li>What's needed is restoration, not just recognition — sets up the whole second half of the week</li>\n<li>Quiet moment: without sharing, let each person silently name (in their own mind or journal) one pattern from today they'd like to see change</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"7c87da0773\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.3 §21 ¶0\" data-sec=\"21\" id=\"p-D.3-21-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.3 §21 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.3 §21 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§21 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 18 — Recap: Day 3 in Review + Questions</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"ba1f1f2dc2\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.3 §21 ¶1\" data-sec=\"21\" id=\"p-D.3-21-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.3 §21 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.3 §21 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§21 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Full recap: behind the Wall of Rejection — the Lust Trap and Flight Swing; behind the Wall of Rebellion — the Pride Trap and Fight Swing; both capable of converging toward the same destructive end if left unaddressed</li>\n<li>Open the floor for questions from today's teaching</li>\n<li>Bridge to tomorrow: \"tomorrow, we stop diagnosing and start rebuilding\"</li>\n<li>Word of encouragement</li>\n<li>Close in prayer</li>\n</ul></div>"}, {"id": "D.4", "title": "Day 4 — Restoration Begins: Revelation and Repentance / Release, Recognize, and Renounce", "order": 5, "metadata": {"source": "src/tdp-day4.md", "kind": "Day 4"}, "file": "d4-day4.md", "notes": [], "blocks": [{"ref": "D.4 §0 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.4-0-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "75d60cb427", "text": "Day 4 — Restoration Begins: Revelation and Repentance / Release, Recognize, and Renounce"}, {"ref": "D.4 §0 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.4-0-1", "kind": "paragraph", "h": "0230d8dd97", "text": "Day overview: Morning session (Part 1) opens with a recap of Day 3, then shifts the whole tone of the week from diagnosis to restoration — the Wall of Salvation, and the first two \"reinforcing rods\": Revelation and Repentance. Afternoon session (Part 2) covers Rod 3 (Release/Forgiveness) and Rods 4–5 (Recognize and Renounce Satan's tactics), leading into personal prayer ministry time."}, {"ref": "D.4 §1 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.4-1-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "76c09890a9", "text": "PART 1 — Restoration Begins: Revelation and Repentance"}, {"ref": "D.4 §1 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.4-1-1", "kind": "paragraph", "h": "b0c95bba78", "text": "Pacing note (≈1.5 hrs): opens with the Day 3 recap and takes any questions that came up during the day. One exercise this session, positioned at the halfway point (Slide 6 of 11) as a physical interruption to refocus — not earlier. Closes with just a quick mention of what Part 2 covers — no full recap yet, questions held for the break."}, {"ref": "D.4 §2 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.4-2-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "2aeec36b3e", "text": "Slide 1 — Yesterday, Quickly — and Any Questions"}, {"ref": "D.4 §2 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.4-2-1", "kind": "list", "h": "a9cff2a584", "text": "Open in prayer One-slide reminder of Day 3: the Lust Trap/Flight Swing behind Rejection; the Pride Trap/Fight Swing behind Rebellion; both able to converge toward the same destructive end if unaddressed Open the floor briefly for any questions that came up for people since yesterday Today's turn: \"today the whole tone changes — from diagnosis to rebuilding\""}, {"ref": "D.4 §3 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.4-3-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "afd5171fae", "text": "Slide 2 — A Change in Direction"}, {"ref": "D.4 §3 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.4-3-1", "kind": "list", "h": "c0e33dfdcf", "text": "Name the shift explicitly: the first three days named the problem; today begins the solution Encourage the room: nothing named this week is beyond repair — that's the whole point of a \"Wall of Salvation\""}, {"ref": "D.4 §4 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.4-4-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "fb41aee2f7", "text": "Slide 3 — The Altar and the Temple"}, {"ref": "D.4 §4 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.4-4-1", "kind": "list", "h": "d71a397fe6", "text": "Introduce the book's imagery: before rebuilding walls, Scripture's pattern is to rebuild the altar and the temple first — worship and God's presence come before structural repair Point: restoration starts with reorienting toward God, not just fixing behavior"}, {"ref": "D.4 §5 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.4-5-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "18cc414606", "text": "Slide 4 — Refined as Gold, Shaped as Clay"}, {"ref": "D.4 §5 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.4-5-1", "kind": "list", "h": "8da0a23146", "text": "Two biblical images for how God works on a person — not the same as demolishing and starting over: Gold refined by fire (Malachi 3:3, 1 Peter 1:7): heat removes dross, but the gold itself isn't destroyed — it's purified Clay shaped by the Potter (Jeremiah 18, Isaiah 64:8): still being formed, still in the Potter's hands — not abandoned to shape itself Apply directly to the wall: protection was good and necessary when the bricks were laid — but some bricks need to go, some need adjusting, some need replacing. That's refining and reshaping, not rejection The key reassurance under both images: God doesn't leave us to ourselves. He stays engaged with the material the whole time"}, {"ref": "D.4 §6 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.4-6-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "227df7e8a3", "text": "Slide 5 — [story about xxx — a moment in your own life where genuine change only began after a shift in your relationship with God, rather than through sheer willpower]"}, {"ref": "D.4 §7 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.4-7-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "efd58d15bf", "text": "Slide 6 — [exercise] Naming One Brick — MIDPOINT BREAK & REFOCUS"}, {"ref": "D.4 §7 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.4-7-1", "kind": "list", "h": "e9d28f1c7f", "text": "Quiet, personal exercise paired with a brief stand-and-stretch: using this week's teaching and questionnaire score, have each person privately write down one specific brick they now see clearly in their own wall Frame it as gold to be refined or clay to be reshaped — not a flaw in who they are Not shared aloud — for the individual's own use in the prayer ministry time later"}, {"ref": "D.4 §8 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.4-8-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "c2b5d531d8", "text": "Slide 7 — The Seven Reinforcing Rods — Overview"}, {"ref": "D.4 §8 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.4-8-1", "kind": "list", "h": "97cd9100b3", "text": "Preview all seven rods briefly so the room can see the whole road ahead: Revelation, Repentance, Release, Recognize, Renounce, Renewing, Rebuilding Today: the first two. Tomorrow: the rest"}, {"ref": "D.4 §9 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.4-9-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "cb6f30e52e", "text": "Slide 8 — Rod 1: Revelation"}, {"ref": "D.4 §9 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.4-9-1", "kind": "list", "h": "3aae72b618", "text": "Define: seeing your wall and its bricks clearly, in God's light rather than in self-condemnation or denial This week's teaching and the questionnaire have already been a form of revelation — today gives it a name and a next step Key distinction: revelation exposes to heal, not to shame — and exposes a brick, not a self"}, {"ref": "D.4 §10 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.4-10-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "759adda230", "text": "Slide 9 — Rod 2: Repentance"}, {"ref": "D.4 §10 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.4-10-1", "kind": "list", "h": "89b4fd3c0f", "text": "Define repentance as a change of mind that leads to a change of direction — not just feeling sorry Rejection's cornerstone: unbelief — repentance here means turning from unbelief about one's identity in God Rebellion's cornerstone: pride — repentance here means turning from self-sufficiency toward dependence on God"}, {"ref": "D.4 §11 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.4-11-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "db35afd821", "text": "Slide 10 — Two Kinds of Repentance, One Destination"}, {"ref": "D.4 §11 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.4-11-1", "kind": "list", "h": "407f95cc05", "text": "Contrast how repentance looks different depending on which wall someone leans toward (Rejection vs. Rebellion) but arrives at the same place: trusting God's plumbline over the false ones named on Day 1 Reinforces the whole week's throughline back to the Amos 7 vision Close with a guided journaling prompt (not a break activity, stays seated): using the brick named earlier, write a short, honest sentence of repentance — not generic, but specific to what was revealed; optional to share with a trusted partner"}, {"ref": "D.4 §12 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.4-12-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "378b53e0d2", "text": "Slide 11 — What's Coming in Part 2"}, {"ref": "D.4 §12 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.4-12-1", "kind": "list", "h": "77973c122e", "text": "Quick, light mention only — no recap yet: after the break, forgiveness (Release) and confronting the enemy's tactics (Recognize/Renounce) Hold questions for the break — we'll pick up discussion after Close in prayer"}, {"ref": "D.4 §13 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.4-13-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "2d3d6b4775", "text": "☕ SHORT BREAK"}, {"ref": "D.4 §14 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.4-14-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "08957f0674", "text": "PART 2 — Release, Recognize, and Renounce"}, {"ref": "D.4 §14 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.4-14-1", "kind": "paragraph", "h": "dfad52220c", "text": "Pacing note (≈1.5 hrs): opens with just a quick mention of what's ahead (and a chance to answer any plenum question from the break) — no recap needed yet. One exercise this session, positioned at the halfway point (Slide 15, the 4th of 8 slides in this Part) as a physical interruption to refocus — not earlier. Closes with the full Day 4 recap and time for questions, leading into Personal Ministry Time."}, {"ref": "D.4 §15 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.4-15-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "34a2ce90cc", "text": "Slide 12 — Opening: What's Next"}, {"ref": "D.4 §15 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.4-15-1", "kind": "list", "h": "9f42c646d6", "text": "Open in prayer Quick, light mention only: \"revelation showed us the brick, repentance turned our heart — now we deal with what's owed to others, and what's coming against us\" If a question came up in the break, take a moment to answer it in plenum here before moving on"}, {"ref": "D.4 §16 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.4-16-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "15aebc3ecc", "text": "Slide 13 — Rod 3: Release (Forgiveness)"}, {"ref": "D.4 §16 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.4-16-1", "kind": "list", "h": "0fe9fcf341", "text": "Define forgiveness biblically: releasing a debt, not excusing an offense or pretending it didn't happen Link back to Day 2's generational pattern teaching: unforgiveness is often exactly how generational wounds get passed on Key reassurance: forgiveness is a decision, not necessarily a felt emotion at first Private journaling prompt (not shared): write down one or two names of people (including possibly yourself) that came to mind during this week's teaching — preparation for today's Personal Ministry Time, between the individual and God, not a public confession"}, {"ref": "D.4 §17 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.4-17-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "46bc97b743", "text": "Slide 14 — [story about xxx — a real forgiveness journey of your own, including how long it took and what changed once you finally released it]"}, {"ref": "D.4 §18 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.4-18-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "421e355494", "text": "Slide 15 — [exercise] Rods 4–5: Recognize and Renounce — MIDPOINT BREAK & REFOCUS"}, {"ref": "D.4 §18 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.4-18-1", "kind": "list", "h": "88df2d0950", "text": "Introduce these as a pair: first recognizing the enemy's tactics, then actively renouncing them Reference the \"four weapons\" or tactics named in the book (deception, accusation, temptation, oppression — adjust to match the actual book language) Stand-and-move moment: a short physical shift (e.g., stand up, shake it off) to mark the halfway point before this heavier topic Point: the goal isn't fear of the enemy, but clarity — an identified tactic loses most of its power"}, {"ref": "D.4 §19 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.4-19-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "0a8893496d", "text": "Slide 16 — What Renouncing Looks Like"}, {"ref": "D.4 §19 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.4-19-1", "kind": "list", "h": "554d5674e0", "text": "Explain renouncing as an active, verbal, decisive break — not just an internal feeling Example structure for a renouncing statement (without being overly scripted/robotic) Reassure: this is a normal, healthy part of the restoration process, not something to fear doing \"wrong\" Practice the language first as a group, using a light, non-personal example (e.g., renouncing a bad habit like procrastination) so people are comfortable with it before applying it personally in private prayer time"}, {"ref": "D.4 §20 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.4-20-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "4a8cb83330", "text": "Slide 17 — Tying the Rods Together"}, {"ref": "D.4 §20 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.4-20-1", "kind": "list", "h": "31df5ae35e", "text": "Quick visual recap of Rods 1–5 in sequence: Revelation → Repentance → Release → Recognize → Renounce Preview: tomorrow completes the picture with Renewing and Rebuilding"}, {"ref": "D.4 §21 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.4-21-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "db180a127a", "text": "Slide 18 — Setting Up Personal Ministry Time"}, {"ref": "D.4 §21 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.4-21-1", "kind": "list", "h": "294fdc4ef5", "text": "Practical instructions for the afternoon's Personal Ministry Time (PMT): quiet space, prayer partners or ministry team availability, confidentiality expectations Encourage using today's private journaling (the named brick, the repentance sentence, the forgiveness list) as the starting material for that time"}, {"ref": "D.4 §22 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.4-22-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "572179104b", "text": "Slide 19 — Recap: Day 4 in Review + Questions"}, {"ref": "D.4 §22 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.4-22-1", "kind": "list", "h": "213b8f4d56", "text": "Full recap: the shift from diagnosis to restoration; the Altar and Temple; Rod 1 Revelation, Rod 2 Repentance, Rod 3 Release/Forgiveness, Rods 4–5 Recognize/Renounce Open the floor for questions from today's teaching Bridge to tomorrow: \"tomorrow we finish the wall — Renewing and Rebuilding, and we close out the week together\" Word of encouragement Close in prayer, transitioning into Personal Ministry Time"}], "html": "<div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"75d60cb427\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.4 §0 ¶0\" data-sec=\"0\" id=\"p-D.4-0-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.4 §0 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.4 §0 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§0 ¶0</button><h1 data-body=\"1\">Day 4 — Restoration Begins: Revelation and Repentance / Release, Recognize, and Renounce</h1></div><div class=\"blk blk-paragraph\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"0230d8dd97\" data-kind=\"paragraph\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.4 §0 ¶1\" data-sec=\"0\" id=\"p-D.4-0-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.4 §0 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.4 §0 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§0 ¶1</button><p data-body=\"1\"><strong>Day overview:</strong> Morning session (Part 1) opens with a recap of Day 3, then shifts the whole tone of the week from diagnosis to restoration — the Wall of Salvation, and the first two \"reinforcing rods\": Revelation and Repentance. Afternoon session (Part 2) covers Rod 3 (Release/Forgiveness) and Rods 4–5 (Recognize and Renounce Satan's tactics), leading into personal prayer ministry time.</p></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"76c09890a9\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.4 §1 ¶0\" data-sec=\"1\" id=\"p-D.4-1-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.4 §1 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.4 §1 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§1 ¶0</button><h2 data-body=\"1\">PART 1 — Restoration Begins: Revelation and Repentance</h2></div><div class=\"blk blk-paragraph\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"b0c95bba78\" data-kind=\"paragraph\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.4 §1 ¶1\" data-sec=\"1\" id=\"p-D.4-1-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.4 §1 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.4 §1 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§1 ¶1</button><p data-body=\"1\"><strong>Pacing note (≈1.5 hrs):</strong> opens with the Day 3 recap and takes any questions that came up during the day. One exercise this session, positioned at the halfway point (Slide 6 of 11) as a physical interruption to refocus — not earlier. Closes with just a quick mention of what Part 2 covers — no full recap yet, questions held for the break.</p></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"2aeec36b3e\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.4 §2 ¶0\" data-sec=\"2\" id=\"p-D.4-2-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.4 §2 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.4 §2 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§2 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 1 — Yesterday, Quickly — and Any Questions</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"a9cff2a584\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.4 §2 ¶1\" data-sec=\"2\" id=\"p-D.4-2-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.4 §2 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.4 §2 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§2 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Open in prayer</li>\n<li>One-slide reminder of Day 3: the Lust Trap/Flight Swing behind Rejection; the Pride Trap/Fight Swing behind Rebellion; both able to converge toward the same destructive end if unaddressed</li>\n<li>Open the floor briefly for any questions that came up for people since yesterday</li>\n<li>Today's turn: \"today the whole tone changes — from diagnosis to rebuilding\"</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"afd5171fae\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.4 §3 ¶0\" data-sec=\"3\" id=\"p-D.4-3-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.4 §3 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.4 §3 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§3 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 2 — A Change in Direction</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"c0e33dfdcf\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.4 §3 ¶1\" data-sec=\"3\" id=\"p-D.4-3-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.4 §3 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.4 §3 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§3 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Name the shift explicitly: the first three days named the problem; today begins the solution</li>\n<li>Encourage the room: nothing named this week is beyond repair — that's the whole point of a \"Wall of Salvation\"</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"fb41aee2f7\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.4 §4 ¶0\" data-sec=\"4\" id=\"p-D.4-4-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.4 §4 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.4 §4 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§4 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 3 — The Altar and the Temple</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"d71a397fe6\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.4 §4 ¶1\" data-sec=\"4\" id=\"p-D.4-4-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.4 §4 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.4 §4 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§4 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Introduce the book's imagery: before rebuilding walls, Scripture's pattern is to rebuild the altar and the temple first — worship and God's presence come before structural repair</li>\n<li>Point: restoration starts with reorienting toward God, not just fixing behavior</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"18cc414606\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.4 §5 ¶0\" data-sec=\"5\" id=\"p-D.4-5-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.4 §5 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.4 §5 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§5 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 4 — Refined as Gold, Shaped as Clay</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"8da0a23146\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.4 §5 ¶1\" data-sec=\"5\" id=\"p-D.4-5-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.4 §5 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.4 §5 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§5 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Two biblical images for how God works on a person — not the same as demolishing and starting over:\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Gold refined by fire</strong> (Malachi 3:3, 1 Peter 1:7): heat removes dross, but the gold itself isn't destroyed — it's purified</li>\n<li><strong>Clay shaped by the Potter</strong> (Jeremiah 18, Isaiah 64:8): still being formed, still in the Potter's hands — not abandoned to shape itself</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Apply directly to the wall: protection was good and necessary when the bricks were laid — but some bricks need to go, some need adjusting, some need replacing. That's refining and reshaping, not rejection</li>\n<li>The key reassurance under both images: <strong>God doesn't leave us to ourselves.</strong> He stays engaged with the material the whole time</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"227df7e8a3\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.4 §6 ¶0\" data-sec=\"6\" id=\"p-D.4-6-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.4 §6 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.4 §6 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§6 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 5 — <span class=\"ph-story\">[story about xxx — a moment in your own life where genuine change only began after a shift in your relationship with God, rather than through sheer willpower]</span></h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"efd58d15bf\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.4 §7 ¶0\" data-sec=\"7\" id=\"p-D.4-7-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.4 §7 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.4 §7 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§7 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 6 — <span class=\"ph-exercise\">[exercise]</span> Naming One Brick — MIDPOINT BREAK &amp; REFOCUS</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"e9d28f1c7f\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.4 §7 ¶1\" data-sec=\"7\" id=\"p-D.4-7-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.4 §7 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.4 §7 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§7 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Quiet, personal exercise paired with a brief stand-and-stretch: using this week's teaching and questionnaire score, have each person privately write down one specific brick they now see clearly in their own wall</li>\n<li>Frame it as gold to be refined or clay to be reshaped — not a flaw in who they are</li>\n<li>Not shared aloud — for the individual's own use in the prayer ministry time later</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"c2b5d531d8\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.4 §8 ¶0\" data-sec=\"8\" id=\"p-D.4-8-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.4 §8 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.4 §8 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§8 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 7 — The Seven Reinforcing Rods — Overview</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"97cd9100b3\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.4 §8 ¶1\" data-sec=\"8\" id=\"p-D.4-8-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.4 §8 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.4 §8 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§8 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Preview all seven rods briefly so the room can see the whole road ahead: Revelation, Repentance, Release, Recognize, Renounce, Renewing, Rebuilding</li>\n<li>Today: the first two. Tomorrow: the rest</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"cb6f30e52e\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.4 §9 ¶0\" data-sec=\"9\" id=\"p-D.4-9-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.4 §9 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.4 §9 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§9 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 8 — Rod 1: Revelation</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"3aae72b618\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.4 §9 ¶1\" data-sec=\"9\" id=\"p-D.4-9-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.4 §9 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.4 §9 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§9 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Define: seeing your wall and its bricks clearly, in God's light rather than in self-condemnation or denial</li>\n<li>This week's teaching and the questionnaire have already been a form of revelation — today gives it a name and a next step</li>\n<li>Key distinction: revelation exposes to heal, not to shame — and exposes a brick, not a self</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"759adda230\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.4 §10 ¶0\" data-sec=\"10\" id=\"p-D.4-10-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.4 §10 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.4 §10 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§10 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 9 — Rod 2: Repentance</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"89b4fd3c0f\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.4 §10 ¶1\" data-sec=\"10\" id=\"p-D.4-10-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.4 §10 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.4 §10 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§10 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Define repentance as a change of mind that leads to a change of direction — not just feeling sorry</li>\n<li>Rejection's cornerstone: unbelief — repentance here means turning from unbelief about one's identity in God</li>\n<li>Rebellion's cornerstone: pride — repentance here means turning from self-sufficiency toward dependence on God</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"db35afd821\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.4 §11 ¶0\" data-sec=\"11\" id=\"p-D.4-11-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.4 §11 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.4 §11 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§11 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 10 — Two Kinds of Repentance, One Destination</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"407f95cc05\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.4 §11 ¶1\" data-sec=\"11\" id=\"p-D.4-11-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.4 §11 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.4 §11 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§11 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Contrast how repentance looks different depending on which wall someone leans toward (Rejection vs. Rebellion) but arrives at the same place: trusting God's plumbline over the false ones named on Day 1</li>\n<li>Reinforces the whole week's throughline back to the Amos 7 vision</li>\n<li>Close with a guided journaling prompt (not a break activity, stays seated): using the brick named earlier, write a short, honest sentence of repentance — not generic, but specific to what was revealed; optional to share with a trusted partner</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"378b53e0d2\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.4 §12 ¶0\" data-sec=\"12\" id=\"p-D.4-12-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.4 §12 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.4 §12 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§12 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 11 — What's Coming in Part 2</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"77973c122e\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.4 §12 ¶1\" data-sec=\"12\" id=\"p-D.4-12-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.4 §12 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.4 §12 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§12 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Quick, light mention only — no recap yet: after the break, forgiveness (Release) and confronting the enemy's tactics (Recognize/Renounce)</li>\n<li>Hold questions for the break — we'll pick up discussion after</li>\n<li>Close in prayer</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"2d3d6b4775\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.4 §13 ¶0\" data-sec=\"13\" id=\"p-D.4-13-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.4 §13 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.4 §13 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§13 ¶0</button><h2 data-body=\"1\">☕ SHORT BREAK</h2></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"08957f0674\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.4 §14 ¶0\" data-sec=\"14\" id=\"p-D.4-14-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.4 §14 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.4 §14 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§14 ¶0</button><h2 data-body=\"1\">PART 2 — Release, Recognize, and Renounce</h2></div><div class=\"blk blk-paragraph\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"dfad52220c\" data-kind=\"paragraph\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.4 §14 ¶1\" data-sec=\"14\" id=\"p-D.4-14-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.4 §14 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.4 §14 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§14 ¶1</button><p data-body=\"1\"><strong>Pacing note (≈1.5 hrs):</strong> opens with just a quick mention of what's ahead (and a chance to answer any plenum question from the break) — no recap needed yet. One exercise this session, positioned at the halfway point (Slide 15, the 4th of 8 slides in this Part) as a physical interruption to refocus — not earlier. Closes with the full Day 4 recap and time for questions, leading into Personal Ministry Time.</p></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"34a2ce90cc\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.4 §15 ¶0\" data-sec=\"15\" id=\"p-D.4-15-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.4 §15 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.4 §15 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§15 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 12 — Opening: What's Next</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"9f42c646d6\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.4 §15 ¶1\" data-sec=\"15\" id=\"p-D.4-15-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.4 §15 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.4 §15 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§15 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Open in prayer</li>\n<li>Quick, light mention only: \"revelation showed us the brick, repentance turned our heart — now we deal with what's owed to others, and what's coming against us\"</li>\n<li>If a question came up in the break, take a moment to answer it in plenum here before moving on</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"15aebc3ecc\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.4 §16 ¶0\" data-sec=\"16\" id=\"p-D.4-16-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.4 §16 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.4 §16 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§16 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 13 — Rod 3: Release (Forgiveness)</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"0fe9fcf341\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.4 §16 ¶1\" data-sec=\"16\" id=\"p-D.4-16-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.4 §16 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.4 §16 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§16 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Define forgiveness biblically: releasing a debt, not excusing an offense or pretending it didn't happen</li>\n<li>Link back to Day 2's generational pattern teaching: unforgiveness is often exactly how generational wounds get passed on</li>\n<li>Key reassurance: forgiveness is a decision, not necessarily a felt emotion at first</li>\n<li>Private journaling prompt (not shared): write down one or two names of people (including possibly yourself) that came to mind during this week's teaching — preparation for today's Personal Ministry Time, between the individual and God, not a public confession</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"46bc97b743\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.4 §17 ¶0\" data-sec=\"17\" id=\"p-D.4-17-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.4 §17 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.4 §17 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§17 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 14 — <span class=\"ph-story\">[story about xxx — a real forgiveness journey of your own, including how long it took and what changed once you finally released it]</span></h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"421e355494\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.4 §18 ¶0\" data-sec=\"18\" id=\"p-D.4-18-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.4 §18 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.4 §18 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§18 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 15 — <span class=\"ph-exercise\">[exercise]</span> Rods 4–5: Recognize and Renounce — MIDPOINT BREAK &amp; REFOCUS</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"88df2d0950\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.4 §18 ¶1\" data-sec=\"18\" id=\"p-D.4-18-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.4 §18 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.4 §18 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§18 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Introduce these as a pair: first recognizing the enemy's tactics, then actively renouncing them</li>\n<li>Reference the \"four weapons\" or tactics named in the book (deception, accusation, temptation, oppression — adjust to match the actual book language)</li>\n<li>Stand-and-move moment: a short physical shift (e.g., stand up, shake it off) to mark the halfway point before this heavier topic</li>\n<li>Point: the goal isn't fear of the enemy, but clarity — an identified tactic loses most of its power</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"0a8893496d\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.4 §19 ¶0\" data-sec=\"19\" id=\"p-D.4-19-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.4 §19 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.4 §19 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§19 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 16 — What Renouncing Looks Like</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"554d5674e0\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.4 §19 ¶1\" data-sec=\"19\" id=\"p-D.4-19-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.4 §19 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.4 §19 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§19 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Explain renouncing as an active, verbal, decisive break — not just an internal feeling</li>\n<li>Example structure for a renouncing statement (without being overly scripted/robotic)</li>\n<li>Reassure: this is a normal, healthy part of the restoration process, not something to fear doing \"wrong\"</li>\n<li>Practice the language first as a group, using a light, non-personal example (e.g., renouncing a bad habit like procrastination) so people are comfortable with it before applying it personally in private prayer time</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"4a8cb83330\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.4 §20 ¶0\" data-sec=\"20\" id=\"p-D.4-20-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.4 §20 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.4 §20 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§20 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 17 — Tying the Rods Together</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"31df5ae35e\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.4 §20 ¶1\" data-sec=\"20\" id=\"p-D.4-20-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.4 §20 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.4 §20 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§20 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Quick visual recap of Rods 1–5 in sequence: Revelation → Repentance → Release → Recognize → Renounce</li>\n<li>Preview: tomorrow completes the picture with Renewing and Rebuilding</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"db180a127a\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.4 §21 ¶0\" data-sec=\"21\" id=\"p-D.4-21-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.4 §21 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.4 §21 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§21 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 18 — Setting Up Personal Ministry Time</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"294fdc4ef5\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.4 §21 ¶1\" data-sec=\"21\" id=\"p-D.4-21-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.4 §21 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.4 §21 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§21 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Practical instructions for the afternoon's Personal Ministry Time (PMT): quiet space, prayer partners or ministry team availability, confidentiality expectations</li>\n<li>Encourage using today's private journaling (the named brick, the repentance sentence, the forgiveness list) as the starting material for that time</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"572179104b\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.4 §22 ¶0\" data-sec=\"22\" id=\"p-D.4-22-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.4 §22 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.4 §22 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§22 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 19 — Recap: Day 4 in Review + Questions</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"213b8f4d56\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.4 §22 ¶1\" data-sec=\"22\" id=\"p-D.4-22-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.4 §22 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.4 §22 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§22 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Full recap: the shift from diagnosis to restoration; the Altar and Temple; Rod 1 Revelation, Rod 2 Repentance, Rod 3 Release/Forgiveness, Rods 4–5 Recognize/Renounce</li>\n<li>Open the floor for questions from today's teaching</li>\n<li>Bridge to tomorrow: \"tomorrow we finish the wall — Renewing and Rebuilding, and we close out the week together\"</li>\n<li>Word of encouragement</li>\n<li>Close in prayer, transitioning into Personal Ministry Time</li>\n</ul></div>"}, {"id": "D.5", "title": "Day 5 — Renewing the Mind / Rebuilding, the Gates of Jerusalem, and Commissioning", "order": 6, "metadata": {"source": "src/tdp-day5.md", "kind": "Day 5"}, "file": "d5-day5.md", "notes": ["<p>original (Q3, 2026-11-16): \"- Full recap of the entire week, not just today: the plumbline → behavior built like a wall → the walls and profiles → behind the walls → restoration through the seven rods → rebuilding and the gates\"</p>\n"], "blocks": [{"ref": "D.5 §0 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.5-0-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "f4903a355e", "text": "Day 5 — Renewing the Mind / Rebuilding, the Gates of Jerusalem, and Commissioning"}, {"ref": "D.5 §0 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.5-0-1", "kind": "paragraph", "h": "3a92100bd1", "text": "Day overview: Morning session (Part 1) opens with a recap of Day 4, then walks through Rod 6 (Renewing) and its eight practical sub-steps in depth. Afternoon session (Part 2) covers Rod 7 (Rebuilding) and the Gates of Jerusalem allegory, followed by the week's closing session — testimonies, re-scoring/reflecting on the questionnaire, and commissioning. This is the final day of the whole program."}, {"ref": "D.5 §1 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.5-1-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "654155a728", "text": "PART 1 — Renewing the Mind"}, {"ref": "D.5 §1 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.5-1-1", "kind": "paragraph", "h": "f52daf2bcd", "text": "Pacing note (≈1.5 hrs): opens with the Day 4 recap and takes any questions that came up during the day. One exercise this session, positioned after the halfway point (Slide 7 of 10) as a physical interruption to refocus. Closes with just a quick mention of what Part 2 covers — no full recap yet, questions held for the break."}, {"ref": "D.5 §2 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.5-2-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "2aeec36b3e", "text": "Slide 1 — Yesterday, Quickly — and Any Questions"}, {"ref": "D.5 §2 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.5-2-1", "kind": "list", "h": "c3bb143c54", "text": "Open in prayer One-slide reminder of Day 4: the Altar and Temple; Rod 1 Revelation, Rod 2 Repentance, Rod 3 Release/Forgiveness, Rods 4–5 Recognize/Renounce; last night's Personal Ministry Time Open the floor briefly for any questions that came up for people since yesterday Today's turn: \"today we finish building the Wall of Salvation — starting with how the mind actually gets renewed\""}, {"ref": "D.5 §3 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.5-3-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "597ce2417e", "text": "Slide 2 — Rod 6: Renewing"}, {"ref": "D.5 §3 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.5-3-1", "kind": "list", "h": "7b20e8988b", "text": "Define renewing the mind (Romans 12:2) — not a one-time event, but an ongoing replacement of old thought patterns with truth Position this as the longest and most practical of the seven rods — eight concrete sub-steps"}, {"ref": "D.5 §4 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.5-4-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "8e5cd77b18", "text": "Slide 3 — The Eight Steps — Overview"}, {"ref": "D.5 §4 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.5-4-1", "kind": "list", "h": "c7dee3ea2b", "text": "List all eight sub-steps up front so the room can see the shape of the whole process: Acknowledging, Believing, Confessing, Desiring, Exercising (the A-C-D-B model), Forgiving, Growing, Helping Note: some of these echo earlier rods (like forgiving) — renewing weaves them into an ongoing daily practice, not just a one-time crisis response"}, {"ref": "D.5 §5 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.5-5-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "a6ca14c273", "text": "Slide 4 — Step 1–2: Acknowledging and Believing"}, {"ref": "D.5 §5 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.5-5-1", "kind": "list", "h": "a6ee187791", "text": "Acknowledging: naming the old belief/thought pattern honestly (connects back to Day 4's Revelation) Believing: actively choosing to trust what God says over what the old pattern says [story about xxx — a specific old belief about yourself you had to consciously replace with what God says, and what that process actually looked like day to day]"}, {"ref": "D.5 §6 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.5-6-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "703c24db27", "text": "Slide 5 — Step 3–4: Confessing and Desiring"}, {"ref": "D.5 §6 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.5-6-1", "kind": "list", "h": "6d298cde5a", "text": "Confessing: speaking the truth out loud, not just thinking it — reinforces the new pattern through voice and hearing Desiring: cultivating an actual want for the change, not just a grudging obligation to change Quick written exercise: writing one \"confession\" statement replacing an old belief with a biblical truth"}, {"ref": "D.5 §7 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.5-7-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "e88625f87a", "text": "Slide 6 — Step 5: Exercising (the A-C-D-B Model)"}, {"ref": "D.5 §7 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.5-7-1", "kind": "list", "h": "565d8a02c7", "text": "Walk through the A-C-D-B model as the book presents it (Acknowledge, Confess, Desire, Believe, in practiced repetition) — a mini-loop for daily practice Frame as a spiritual \"muscle memory\" exercise — repetition over time, not instant transformation"}, {"ref": "D.5 §8 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.5-8-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "c9c7e5d60d", "text": "Slide 7 — [exercise] Practice the A-C-D-B Loop — MIDPOINT BREAK & REFOCUS"}, {"ref": "D.5 §8 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.5-8-1", "kind": "list", "h": "4daa6f661c", "text": "Walk the whole room through one full round of the A-C-D-B loop together, using a shared, low-stakes example truth (e.g., replacing \"I have to earn love\" with \"I am already loved\") Then invite people to privately run the loop once more with their own specific truth from earlier in the week Good active, participatory break after the halfway point of the session"}, {"ref": "D.5 §9 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.5-9-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "3cbe462f13", "text": "Slide 8 — Step 6–8: Forgiving, Growing, Helping"}, {"ref": "D.5 §9 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.5-9-1", "kind": "list", "h": "6f372de9f3", "text": "Forgiving: an ongoing posture, not a one-time event (ties back to Rod 3) Growing: renewing is a lifelong process, not a finish line reached this week Helping: healed people help others — turning personal restoration outward toward serving others"}, {"ref": "D.5 §10 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.5-10-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "7d6c33b4b7", "text": "Slide 9 — [story about xxx — an example of someone (could be yourself) whose own healing overflowed into helping someone else walk the same path]"}, {"ref": "D.5 §11 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.5-11-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "5667bcf453", "text": "Slide 10 — What's Coming in Part 2"}, {"ref": "D.5 §11 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.5-11-1", "kind": "list", "h": "ef580058a0", "text": "Quick, light mention only — no recap yet: after the break, the final rod — Rebuilding — and the Gates of Jerusalem, followed by the week's closing session Hold questions for the break — we'll pick up discussion after Close in prayer"}, {"ref": "D.5 §12 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.5-12-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "2d3d6b4775", "text": "☕ SHORT BREAK"}, {"ref": "D.5 §13 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.5-13-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "e7b5baad80", "text": "PART 2 — Rebuilding, the Gates of Jerusalem, and Commissioning"}, {"ref": "D.5 §13 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.5-13-1", "kind": "paragraph", "h": "aa0e6a8a46", "text": "Pacing note (≈1.5 hrs): opens with just a quick mention of what's ahead (and a chance to answer any plenum question from the break) — no recap needed yet. One exercise this session, positioned at the halfway point (Slide 16, the 6th of 11 slides in this Part) as a physical interruption to refocus — not earlier. Closes with the entire week's recap (not just today's) and dedicated time for encouragements, not just questions. This is the final session of the whole program."}, {"ref": "D.5 §14 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.5-14-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "ee947e0817", "text": "Slide 11 — Opening: What's Next"}, {"ref": "D.5 §14 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.5-14-1", "kind": "list", "h": "d2fa9d818f", "text": "Open in prayer Quick, light mention only: \"we've renewed the mind — now, in our last session together, we finish the wall itself\" If a question came up in the break, take a moment to answer it in plenum here before moving on"}, {"ref": "D.5 §15 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.5-15-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "ffc6f1dcb1", "text": "Slide 12 — Rod 7: Rebuilding"}, {"ref": "D.5 §15 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.5-15-1", "kind": "list", "h": "9fd6deb11c", "text": "Define rebuilding as the practical, ongoing construction of a new, healthy wall — bricks now laid on the true plumbline (Christ) instead of the false prophets from Day 1 Point back to Nehemiah's rebuilding of Jerusalem's actual walls as the scriptural backbone for this final rod"}, {"ref": "D.5 §16 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.5-16-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "0be6027f0b", "text": "Slide 13 — The Gates of Jerusalem — Introduction"}, {"ref": "D.5 §16 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.5-16-1", "kind": "list", "h": "1f69980c22", "text": "Introduce Nehemiah's ten gates as an allegory for different aspects of restored life Brief list of the gates: Sheep, Fish, Old, Valley, Rubbish, Fountain, Water, Horse, East, Watch"}, {"ref": "D.5 §17 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.5-17-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "c8486b5579", "text": "Slide 14 — The Gates in Depth (Part 1)"}, {"ref": "D.5 §17 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.5-17-1", "kind": "list", "h": "4d84400249", "text": "Sheep Gate (sacrifice/relationship with God), Fish Gate (evangelism/mission), Old Gate (tradition/heritage), Valley Gate (humility) Brief meaning for each, tied back to practical daily life"}, {"ref": "D.5 §18 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.5-18-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "d32da14865", "text": "Slide 15 — The Gates in Depth (Part 2)"}, {"ref": "D.5 §18 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.5-18-1", "kind": "list", "h": "f87bff46b3", "text": "Rubbish Gate (dealing with what needs to be discarded), Fountain Gate (the Holy Spirit/refreshing), Water Gate (the Word of God), Horse Gate (spiritual warfare/strength) Continue brief meanings tied to practical life"}, {"ref": "D.5 §19 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.5-19-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "1014fc665c", "text": "Slide 16 — [exercise] Match the Gate to Its Meaning — MIDPOINT BREAK & REFOCUS"}, {"ref": "D.5 §19 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.5-19-1", "kind": "list", "h": "daf5253b19", "text": "Quick, energetic group activity: shuffle cards or slips with gate names and meanings, have teams race to match them correctly Good active break at the halfway point of this final session, before moving into the closing material"}, {"ref": "D.5 §20 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.5-20-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "62e978344d", "text": "Slide 17 — The Gates in Depth (Part 3)"}, {"ref": "D.5 §20 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.5-20-1", "kind": "list", "h": "4e06cf7fe2", "text": "East Gate (the Lord's return/hope), Watch Gate (vigilance/perseverance) Close the allegory: a rebuilt wall isn't just repaired — it's a whole functioning city again, open to the right traffic and guarded against the wrong"}, {"ref": "D.5 §21 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.5-21-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "c39fa72816", "text": "Slide 18 — [story about xxx — an image or moment of your own \"rebuilding\" becoming visible to others, something concrete that changed once the inner wall was restored]"}, {"ref": "D.5 §22 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.5-22-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "48ff29137d", "text": "Slide 19 — The Character of the King of Glory"}, {"ref": "D.5 §22 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.5-22-1", "kind": "list", "h": "056667f10e", "text": "Close the teaching portion of the week with Psalm 24's \"King of Glory\" image — the one the gates are ultimately opened for Tie back to the entire week's throughline: from a crooked wall measured against a false plumbline, to a rebuilt wall measured against Christ Invite a quiet, optional reflection (not a break activity): look back at your Day 2 questionnaire results — not a formal re-take, just noticing what feels different after one week; one week won't erase a lifetime pattern, but naming a starting point matters"}, {"ref": "D.5 §23 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.5-23-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "b6a4b15aa6", "text": "Slide 20 — Recap: The Whole Week + Time for Encouragements"}, {"ref": "D.5 §23 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.5-23-1", "kind": "list", "h": "5c4c74c176", "text": "Full recap of the entire week, not just today: the plumbline → behavior built like a wall → the walls and situations → behind the walls → restoration through the seven rods → rebuilding and the gates This is the final session, so give this its own real time — not just a question slot: invite testimonies and words of encouragement for one another, not only questions Optional: invite one or two volunteers to briefly share what stood out to them this week"}, {"ref": "D.5 §24 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.5-24-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "a1e95f455d", "text": "Slide 21 — Closing and Commissioning"}, {"ref": "D.5 §24 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.5-24-1", "kind": "list", "h": "c1fc13a9ea", "text": "Final encouraging word: this week is a beginning, not a finish line Echo Day 4's images one last time: God is still refining as gold and shaping as clay — He doesn't leave anyone to themselves, and the work continues long after this week ends [story about xxx — a closing word of encouragement drawn from your own long-term experience of walking out this material, to send the group off well] Close in prayer — a commissioning prayer/blessing over the group as they carry this into their DTS outreach and beyond"}], "html": "<div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"f4903a355e\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.5 §0 ¶0\" data-sec=\"0\" id=\"p-D.5-0-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.5 §0 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.5 §0 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§0 ¶0</button><h1 data-body=\"1\">Day 5 — Renewing the Mind / Rebuilding, the Gates of Jerusalem, and Commissioning</h1></div><div class=\"blk blk-paragraph\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"3a92100bd1\" data-kind=\"paragraph\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.5 §0 ¶1\" data-sec=\"0\" id=\"p-D.5-0-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.5 §0 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.5 §0 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§0 ¶1</button><p data-body=\"1\"><strong>Day overview:</strong> Morning session (Part 1) opens with a recap of Day 4, then walks through Rod 6 (Renewing) and its eight practical sub-steps in depth. Afternoon session (Part 2) covers Rod 7 (Rebuilding) and the Gates of Jerusalem allegory, followed by the week's closing session — testimonies, re-scoring/reflecting on the questionnaire, and commissioning. This is the final day of the whole program.</p></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"654155a728\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.5 §1 ¶0\" data-sec=\"1\" id=\"p-D.5-1-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.5 §1 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.5 §1 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§1 ¶0</button><h2 data-body=\"1\">PART 1 — Renewing the Mind</h2></div><div class=\"blk blk-paragraph\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"f52daf2bcd\" data-kind=\"paragraph\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.5 §1 ¶1\" data-sec=\"1\" id=\"p-D.5-1-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.5 §1 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.5 §1 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§1 ¶1</button><p data-body=\"1\"><strong>Pacing note (≈1.5 hrs):</strong> opens with the Day 4 recap and takes any questions that came up during the day. One exercise this session, positioned after the halfway point (Slide 7 of 10) as a physical interruption to refocus. Closes with just a quick mention of what Part 2 covers — no full recap yet, questions held for the break.</p></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"2aeec36b3e\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.5 §2 ¶0\" data-sec=\"2\" id=\"p-D.5-2-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.5 §2 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.5 §2 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§2 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 1 — Yesterday, Quickly — and Any Questions</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"c3bb143c54\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.5 §2 ¶1\" data-sec=\"2\" id=\"p-D.5-2-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.5 §2 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.5 §2 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§2 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Open in prayer</li>\n<li>One-slide reminder of Day 4: the Altar and Temple; Rod 1 Revelation, Rod 2 Repentance, Rod 3 Release/Forgiveness, Rods 4–5 Recognize/Renounce; last night's Personal Ministry Time</li>\n<li>Open the floor briefly for any questions that came up for people since yesterday</li>\n<li>Today's turn: \"today we finish building the Wall of Salvation — starting with how the mind actually gets renewed\"</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"597ce2417e\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.5 §3 ¶0\" data-sec=\"3\" id=\"p-D.5-3-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.5 §3 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.5 §3 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§3 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 2 — Rod 6: Renewing</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"7b20e8988b\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.5 §3 ¶1\" data-sec=\"3\" id=\"p-D.5-3-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.5 §3 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.5 §3 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§3 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Define renewing the mind (Romans 12:2) — not a one-time event, but an ongoing replacement of old thought patterns with truth</li>\n<li>Position this as the longest and most practical of the seven rods — eight concrete sub-steps</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"8e5cd77b18\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.5 §4 ¶0\" data-sec=\"4\" id=\"p-D.5-4-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.5 §4 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.5 §4 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§4 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 3 — The Eight Steps — Overview</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"c7dee3ea2b\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.5 §4 ¶1\" data-sec=\"4\" id=\"p-D.5-4-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.5 §4 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.5 §4 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§4 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>List all eight sub-steps up front so the room can see the shape of the whole process: Acknowledging, Believing, Confessing, Desiring, Exercising (the A-C-D-B model), Forgiving, Growing, Helping</li>\n<li>Note: some of these echo earlier rods (like forgiving) — renewing weaves them into an ongoing daily practice, not just a one-time crisis response</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"a6ca14c273\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.5 §5 ¶0\" data-sec=\"5\" id=\"p-D.5-5-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.5 §5 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.5 §5 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§5 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 4 — Step 1–2: Acknowledging and Believing</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"a6ee187791\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.5 §5 ¶1\" data-sec=\"5\" id=\"p-D.5-5-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.5 §5 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.5 §5 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§5 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Acknowledging: naming the old belief/thought pattern honestly (connects back to Day 4's Revelation)</li>\n<li>Believing: actively choosing to trust what God says over what the old pattern says</li>\n<li><span class=\"ph-story\">[story about xxx — a specific old belief about yourself you had to consciously replace with what God says, and what that process actually looked like day to day]</span></li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"703c24db27\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.5 §6 ¶0\" data-sec=\"6\" id=\"p-D.5-6-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.5 §6 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.5 §6 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§6 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 5 — Step 3–4: Confessing and Desiring</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"6d298cde5a\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.5 §6 ¶1\" data-sec=\"6\" id=\"p-D.5-6-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.5 §6 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.5 §6 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§6 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Confessing: speaking the truth out loud, not just thinking it — reinforces the new pattern through voice and hearing</li>\n<li>Desiring: cultivating an actual want for the change, not just a grudging obligation to change</li>\n<li>Quick written exercise: writing one \"confession\" statement replacing an old belief with a biblical truth</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"e88625f87a\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.5 §7 ¶0\" data-sec=\"7\" id=\"p-D.5-7-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.5 §7 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.5 §7 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§7 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 6 — Step 5: Exercising (the A-C-D-B Model)</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"565d8a02c7\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.5 §7 ¶1\" data-sec=\"7\" id=\"p-D.5-7-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.5 §7 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.5 §7 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§7 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Walk through the A-C-D-B model as the book presents it (Acknowledge, Confess, Desire, Believe, in practiced repetition) — a mini-loop for daily practice</li>\n<li>Frame as a spiritual \"muscle memory\" exercise — repetition over time, not instant transformation</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"c9c7e5d60d\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.5 §8 ¶0\" data-sec=\"8\" id=\"p-D.5-8-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.5 §8 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.5 §8 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§8 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 7 — <span class=\"ph-exercise\">[exercise]</span> Practice the A-C-D-B Loop — MIDPOINT BREAK &amp; REFOCUS</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"4daa6f661c\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.5 §8 ¶1\" data-sec=\"8\" id=\"p-D.5-8-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.5 §8 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.5 §8 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§8 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Walk the whole room through one full round of the A-C-D-B loop together, using a shared, low-stakes example truth (e.g., replacing \"I have to earn love\" with \"I am already loved\")</li>\n<li>Then invite people to privately run the loop once more with their own specific truth from earlier in the week</li>\n<li>Good active, participatory break after the halfway point of the session</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"3cbe462f13\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.5 §9 ¶0\" data-sec=\"9\" id=\"p-D.5-9-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.5 §9 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.5 §9 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§9 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 8 — Step 6–8: Forgiving, Growing, Helping</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"6f372de9f3\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.5 §9 ¶1\" data-sec=\"9\" id=\"p-D.5-9-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.5 §9 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.5 §9 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§9 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Forgiving: an ongoing posture, not a one-time event (ties back to Rod 3)</li>\n<li>Growing: renewing is a lifelong process, not a finish line reached this week</li>\n<li>Helping: healed people help others — turning personal restoration outward toward serving others</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"7d6c33b4b7\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.5 §10 ¶0\" data-sec=\"10\" id=\"p-D.5-10-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.5 §10 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.5 §10 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§10 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 9 — <span class=\"ph-story\">[story about xxx — an example of someone (could be yourself) whose own healing overflowed into helping someone else walk the same path]</span></h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"5667bcf453\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.5 §11 ¶0\" data-sec=\"11\" id=\"p-D.5-11-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.5 §11 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.5 §11 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§11 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 10 — What's Coming in Part 2</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"ef580058a0\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.5 §11 ¶1\" data-sec=\"11\" id=\"p-D.5-11-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.5 §11 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.5 §11 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§11 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Quick, light mention only — no recap yet: after the break, the final rod — Rebuilding — and the Gates of Jerusalem, followed by the week's closing session</li>\n<li>Hold questions for the break — we'll pick up discussion after</li>\n<li>Close in prayer</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"2d3d6b4775\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.5 §12 ¶0\" data-sec=\"12\" id=\"p-D.5-12-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.5 §12 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.5 §12 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§12 ¶0</button><h2 data-body=\"1\">☕ SHORT BREAK</h2></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"e7b5baad80\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.5 §13 ¶0\" data-sec=\"13\" id=\"p-D.5-13-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.5 §13 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.5 §13 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§13 ¶0</button><h2 data-body=\"1\">PART 2 — Rebuilding, the Gates of Jerusalem, and Commissioning</h2></div><div class=\"blk blk-paragraph\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"aa0e6a8a46\" data-kind=\"paragraph\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.5 §13 ¶1\" data-sec=\"13\" id=\"p-D.5-13-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.5 §13 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.5 §13 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§13 ¶1</button><p data-body=\"1\"><strong>Pacing note (≈1.5 hrs):</strong> opens with just a quick mention of what's ahead (and a chance to answer any plenum question from the break) — no recap needed yet. One exercise this session, positioned at the halfway point (Slide 16, the 6th of 11 slides in this Part) as a physical interruption to refocus — not earlier. Closes with the entire week's recap (not just today's) and dedicated time for encouragements, not just questions. This is the final session of the whole program.</p></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"ee947e0817\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.5 §14 ¶0\" data-sec=\"14\" id=\"p-D.5-14-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.5 §14 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.5 §14 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§14 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 11 — Opening: What's Next</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"d2fa9d818f\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.5 §14 ¶1\" data-sec=\"14\" id=\"p-D.5-14-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.5 §14 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.5 §14 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§14 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Open in prayer</li>\n<li>Quick, light mention only: \"we've renewed the mind — now, in our last session together, we finish the wall itself\"</li>\n<li>If a question came up in the break, take a moment to answer it in plenum here before moving on</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"ffc6f1dcb1\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.5 §15 ¶0\" data-sec=\"15\" id=\"p-D.5-15-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.5 §15 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.5 §15 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§15 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 12 — Rod 7: Rebuilding</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"9fd6deb11c\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.5 §15 ¶1\" data-sec=\"15\" id=\"p-D.5-15-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.5 §15 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.5 §15 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§15 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Define rebuilding as the practical, ongoing construction of a new, healthy wall — bricks now laid on the true plumbline (Christ) instead of the false prophets from Day 1</li>\n<li>Point back to Nehemiah's rebuilding of Jerusalem's actual walls as the scriptural backbone for this final rod</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"0be6027f0b\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.5 §16 ¶0\" data-sec=\"16\" id=\"p-D.5-16-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.5 §16 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.5 §16 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§16 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 13 — The Gates of Jerusalem — Introduction</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"1f69980c22\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.5 §16 ¶1\" data-sec=\"16\" id=\"p-D.5-16-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.5 §16 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.5 §16 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§16 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Introduce Nehemiah's ten gates as an allegory for different aspects of restored life</li>\n<li>Brief list of the gates: Sheep, Fish, Old, Valley, Rubbish, Fountain, Water, Horse, East, Watch</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"c8486b5579\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.5 §17 ¶0\" data-sec=\"17\" id=\"p-D.5-17-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.5 §17 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.5 §17 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§17 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 14 — The Gates in Depth (Part 1)</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"4d84400249\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.5 §17 ¶1\" data-sec=\"17\" id=\"p-D.5-17-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.5 §17 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.5 §17 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§17 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Sheep Gate (sacrifice/relationship with God), Fish Gate (evangelism/mission), Old Gate (tradition/heritage), Valley Gate (humility)</li>\n<li>Brief meaning for each, tied back to practical daily life</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"d32da14865\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.5 §18 ¶0\" data-sec=\"18\" id=\"p-D.5-18-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.5 §18 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.5 §18 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§18 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 15 — The Gates in Depth (Part 2)</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"f87bff46b3\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.5 §18 ¶1\" data-sec=\"18\" id=\"p-D.5-18-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.5 §18 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.5 §18 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§18 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Rubbish Gate (dealing with what needs to be discarded), Fountain Gate (the Holy Spirit/refreshing), Water Gate (the Word of God), Horse Gate (spiritual warfare/strength)</li>\n<li>Continue brief meanings tied to practical life</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"1014fc665c\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.5 §19 ¶0\" data-sec=\"19\" id=\"p-D.5-19-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.5 §19 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.5 §19 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§19 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 16 — <span class=\"ph-exercise\">[exercise]</span> Match the Gate to Its Meaning — MIDPOINT BREAK &amp; REFOCUS</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"daf5253b19\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.5 §19 ¶1\" data-sec=\"19\" id=\"p-D.5-19-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.5 §19 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.5 §19 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§19 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Quick, energetic group activity: shuffle cards or slips with gate names and meanings, have teams race to match them correctly</li>\n<li>Good active break at the halfway point of this final session, before moving into the closing material</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"62e978344d\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.5 §20 ¶0\" data-sec=\"20\" id=\"p-D.5-20-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.5 §20 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.5 §20 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§20 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 17 — The Gates in Depth (Part 3)</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"4e06cf7fe2\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.5 §20 ¶1\" data-sec=\"20\" id=\"p-D.5-20-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.5 §20 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.5 §20 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§20 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>East Gate (the Lord's return/hope), Watch Gate (vigilance/perseverance)</li>\n<li>Close the allegory: a rebuilt wall isn't just repaired — it's a whole functioning city again, open to the right traffic and guarded against the wrong</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"c39fa72816\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.5 §21 ¶0\" data-sec=\"21\" id=\"p-D.5-21-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.5 §21 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.5 §21 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§21 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 18 — <span class=\"ph-story\">[story about xxx — an image or moment of your own \"rebuilding\" becoming visible to others, something concrete that changed once the inner wall was restored]</span></h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"48ff29137d\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.5 §22 ¶0\" data-sec=\"22\" id=\"p-D.5-22-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.5 §22 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.5 §22 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§22 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 19 — The Character of the King of Glory</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"056667f10e\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.5 §22 ¶1\" data-sec=\"22\" id=\"p-D.5-22-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.5 §22 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.5 §22 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§22 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Close the teaching portion of the week with Psalm 24's \"King of Glory\" image — the one the gates are ultimately opened for</li>\n<li>Tie back to the entire week's throughline: from a crooked wall measured against a false plumbline, to a rebuilt wall measured against Christ</li>\n<li>Invite a quiet, optional reflection (not a break activity): look back at your Day 2 questionnaire results — not a formal re-take, just noticing what feels different after one week; one week won't erase a lifetime pattern, but naming a starting point matters</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"b6a4b15aa6\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.5 §23 ¶0\" data-sec=\"23\" id=\"p-D.5-23-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.5 §23 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.5 §23 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§23 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 20 — Recap: The Whole Week + Time for Encouragements</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"5c4c74c176\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.5 §23 ¶1\" data-sec=\"23\" id=\"p-D.5-23-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.5 §23 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.5 §23 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§23 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Full recap of the entire week, not just today: the plumbline → behavior built like a wall → the walls and situations → behind the walls → restoration through the seven rods → rebuilding and the gates</li>\n<li>This is the final session, so give this its own real time — not just a question slot: invite testimonies and words of encouragement for one another, not only questions</li>\n<li>Optional: invite one or two volunteers to briefly share what stood out to them this week</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"a1e95f455d\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.5 §24 ¶0\" data-sec=\"24\" id=\"p-D.5-24-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.5 §24 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.5 §24 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§24 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 21 — Closing and Commissioning</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"c1fc13a9ea\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.5 §24 ¶1\" data-sec=\"24\" id=\"p-D.5-24-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.5 §24 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.5 §24 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§24 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Final encouraging word: this week is a beginning, not a finish line</li>\n<li>Echo Day 4's images one last time: God is still refining as gold and shaping as clay — He doesn't leave anyone to themselves, and the work continues long after this week ends</li>\n<li><span class=\"ph-story\">[story about xxx — a closing word of encouragement drawn from your own long-term experience of walking out this material, to send the group off well]</span></li>\n<li>Close in prayer — a commissioning prayer/blessing over the group as they carry this into their DTS outreach and beyond</li>\n</ul></div>"}], "pages": {"help": "<h1>Help</h1>\n<p>This reader shows <strong>The Divine Plumbline</strong> teaching material: an overview, the 5-day plan and the five day texts. You can read it, change it in place, propose removals and leave comments. Your feedback stays in this browser until you export it.</p>\n<h2>Parts and references</h2>\n<table class=\"table table-sm table-bordered\">\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Part</th>\n<th>What</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>O.1</strong></td>\n<td>Overview: a summary of the week, two sections per day (the reader opens here)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>P.1</strong></td>\n<td>The 5-day DTS program (plan for the week)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>D.1 – D.5</strong></td>\n<td>Day 1 to Day 5: slide-by-slide teaching text</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Every block has a reference in the margin, like <strong>§3 ¶4</strong>. The full reference is <code>PART §section ¶paragraph</code>, for example <code>D.2 §3 ¶4</code>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>§0</strong> is the part title and the text before the first heading (the day overview).</li>\n<li>Every heading starts a new section and is <strong>¶0</strong> of it: <em>PART 1</em>, each <em>Slide</em>, <em>☕ SHORT BREAK</em>, <em>PART 2</em>; in the plan each <em>DAY</em>.</li>\n<li><strong>¶</strong> counts the blocks after the heading: usually ¶1 is the bullet list of a slide, ¶2 a delivery note under it.</li>\n<li>Section numbers are not slide numbers. In Day 1, <em>Slide 1</em> is §2 because <em>PART 1</em> is §1. The § ¶ badge in the margin always shows the right one.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Click a reference to copy it and to show the action buttons (useful on a phone). In chat you can write for example: <em>\"D.1 §5 ¶1: shorten this list\"</em> (the bullet list of Slide 4).</p>\n<h2>Reading</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Parts</strong> on the left (the ☰ button on a phone). Previous / next links are at the bottom of each part.</li>\n<li><strong>Dark / light</strong>: the moon / sun button. The choice is remembered in this browser.</li>\n<li>Highlighted in yellow: <strong>[story about xxx …]</strong> placeholders, where the presenter tells a story of their own. Green: <strong>[exercise]</strong> tags.</li>\n<li>On a phone, the <strong>sliders</strong> button at the top right shows the title, tabs and the editing switch.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Editing a block</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Click a paragraph, heading or list (or the ✎ button). It turns yellow.</li>\n<li>Type your change. <strong>Enter</strong> saves (in lists Enter starts a new item; use <strong>Ctrl+Enter</strong> to save). <strong>Shift+Enter</strong> makes a line break. <strong>Esc</strong> cancels.</li>\n<li><strong>Save</strong>, <strong>Cancel</strong> or <strong>Reset to source</strong>. An edited block is marked <em>Edited</em>; <em>Show original</em> lets you compare.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Only one block is edited at a time. Turn <strong>Editing off</strong> in the top bar to read without accidental edits. Links still work with a normal click.</p>\n<p>If the source text changes after you edited it (a new build), the block shows <strong>Source changed since…</strong> and your edit is not applied. You can <em>Edit again</em> or <em>Discard</em>.</p>\n<h2>Removing and commenting</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>🗑 <strong>Propose removal</strong>: the block is struck through. <strong>Restore</strong> undoes it. The original text is kept for the export.</li>\n<li>💬 <strong>Comment</strong>: write a note; it appears under the block with a blue bar.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>My feedback and export</h2>\n<p>The <strong>My feedback</strong> tab lists all your changes, removals and comments by part, with links back to the text. Each item has <strong>Undo</strong> / <strong>Delete</strong>. <strong>Clear all</strong> asks first.</p>\n<p>Export with <strong>Copy to clipboard</strong>, <strong>Download .md</strong> or <strong>Download .json</strong>, and paste or send it back in the chat. The Markdown looks like this:</p>\n<pre><code># Feedback on The Divine Plumbline\nExported 2026-11-15 14:05 · reader built 2026-11-15 10:00 · reader v1.4.0\nContent: 1 changes, 1 removed, 1 comments\n## D.1 Day 1 — Welcome &amp; The Plumbline / Truth and Deception\n### D.1 §5 ¶1 — CHANGED\nBefore: \"old text\"\nAfter: \"new text\"\n### D.1 §6 ¶1 — REMOVED\nBefore: \"text\"\n- D.1 §14 ¶1 — COMMENT: my comment\n  &gt; «start of the block»\n</code></pre>\n<p>A list edit is exported as the whole list, Before and After.</p>\n<h2>Editor notes (for authors)</h2>\n<p>An HTML comment <code>&lt;!-- … --&gt;</code> or a fenced block with the language <code>EDITOR-NOTE</code> is hidden from the text and shown in an <strong>Editor notes</strong> panel next to it.</p>\n<h2>Privacy</h2>\n<p>Nothing is sent anywhere. Your feedback is stored in this browser's local storage. Clearing browser data deletes it, so export first. The link is unlisted but not secret: anyone who has it can read this copy.</p>\n<hr/>\n<p>reader v1.4.0 · built 2026-11-17 15:52</p>\n"}, "stats": {"parts": 7, "blocks": 277, "notes": 24, "links_internal": 5, "links_external": 0, "placeholders": 23}};
